window.DECKHAND_DOCS_INDEX=[{"page":"Overview","section":"Getting started","heading":"Overview","url":"index.html","text":"Deckhand is a Windows file organiser. You give it a folder and a rule. It finds the files that match, shows you what would happen, and then moves, renames, sorts, copies, zips or extracts them. It can run once, watch a folder for new files, or follow a schedule. The Home page is the first thing you see. It has counters for the files handled, a box that runs a preset on a folder, the rules that need attention and your latest runs. The Rules page lists every rule in groups, in run order, with the actions, the watched folders and a live status."},{"page":"Overview","section":"Getting started","heading":"The idea in four steps","url":"index.html#the-idea-in-four-steps","text":"Pick a folder to watch, such as your Downloads. Say which files with conditions: name, type, dates, size, metadata or contents. Say what happens with actions: move, rename, sort into folders, copy, zip, extract, recycle and more. Preview, then run. Nothing changes until you press Run, and most runs can be undone from History. Deckhand is in beta. Keep backups of important files, especially before trying rules that delete or overwrite."},{"page":"Overview","section":"Getting started","heading":"What you will see in the app","url":"index.html#what-you-will-see-in-the-app","text":"The sidebar in Deckhand has pages for the things you do most. This documentation uses these names. Page What it is for **Home** A quick look at what is running and your recent runs. **Rules** Rules that watch folders and handle matching files. **Presets** Saved tidy-ups you run on a folder you choose. **Schedules** Everything that runs by itself at a set time. **History** Every run and every action, with Undo, plus a searchable Log. **Simulate** Follow an example file through your rules without changing anything. **Diagnostics** What Deckhand costs in CPU and memory, and how long each rule takes. **Settings** Appearance, safety defaults, the OCR language, backup, the Explorer menu, AI integrations and the link to this documentation. See [Settings](settings.html). The exact list and order can differ between releases. If a page here does not match what you see, check the in-app Help."},{"page":"Install","section":"Getting started","heading":"Install","url":"install.html","text":"Deckhand runs on 64-bit Windows 10 and 11. It needs the Microsoft Edge WebView2 Runtime, which is already present on most up-to-date PCs."},{"page":"Install","section":"Getting started","heading":"Requirements","url":"install.html#requirements","text":"64-bit Windows 10 or 11. Microsoft Edge WebView2 Runtime. If it is missing, install the Evergreen Runtime from the WebView2 download page."},{"page":"Install","section":"Getting started","heading":"Choose how to install","url":"install.html#choose-how-to-install","text":"Option Best for Updates [Microsoft Store](https://apps.microsoft.com/detail/9p5314gfs8s6) Most people. The signed version. Automatic [GitHub release](https://github.com/AuZzii-Xssistant/Deckhand/releases/latest) (portable EXE) A free portable build you keep in a folder of your choice. Manual: download the new EXE"},{"page":"Install","section":"Getting started","heading":"Microsoft Store","url":"install.html#microsoft-store","text":"Open the Deckhand page in the Microsoft Store. Select Get and wait for the install to finish. Open Deckhand from the Start menu."},{"page":"Install","section":"Getting started","heading":"Portable EXE from GitHub","url":"install.html#portable-exe-from-github","text":"Open the latest release and download Deckhand.exe. Download the SHA-256 checksum from the same release and compare it with your file (see below). Move Deckhand.exe to a folder you intend to keep, then run it. The portable executable is unsigned. Windows SmartScreen may show an unknown-publisher or unrecognised-app warning. Verify the download against the release checksum before you decide whether to run it. To check the file in PowerShell, run this in the folder that holds the download and compare the result with the published checksum: Get-FileHash .\\Deckhand.exe -Algorithm SHA256 If the search finds nothing, use the Store or the GitHub release."},{"page":"Install","section":"Getting started","heading":"First launch","url":"install.html#first-launch","text":"Open Deckhand. It starts on Home. Do not point a new rule at an important folder yet. Make a test folder with a few sample files. Continue with Your first rule. On a first start Deckhand shows the Home page with nothing watching yet, as in the screenshot: the counters are at zero and the lists say No rules yet and No runs yet. Shortcuts offer Juggle now, New rule and the Command palette, and the Run a preset box works straight away. The Rules page of a fresh install says No rules yet and offers Add rule and New group. Help is built in. The book button in the title bar, Open the docs in Settings and Online docs on the Help page all open this documentation in your browser. See Settings."},{"page":"Install","section":"Getting started","heading":"Updating","url":"install.html#updating","text":"Store: updates arrive automatically. Portable EXE: download the new Deckhand.exe from the releases page and replace the old one. Back up your library first from Settings → Backup if you want a safety copy."},{"page":"Your first rule","section":"Getting started","heading":"Your first rule","url":"first-rule.html","text":"This walk-through builds one rule, Invoices to Documents: PDF invoices that land in Downloads move into Documents\\Invoices, one folder per year. You preview it first, run it once, and undo it afterwards. Every screenshot on this page shows that same rule, in order. Practise on a test folder first. Put a few sample PDFs in a folder such as Documents\\DeckhandTest, and use that as the watch folder until you trust the rule."},{"page":"Your first rule","section":"Getting started","heading":"1. Open the rule editor","url":"first-rule.html#1-open-the-rule-editor","text":"Open Rules and add a new rule. The Guided view walks you through four steps: Where and when, Which files, What happens and Preview. Type the rule's name in Description. Here it is Invoices to Documents. Button and field names can differ slightly between releases. If you prefer to see everything at once, switch to Full editor. See Guided and Full editor."},{"page":"Your first rule","section":"Getting started","heading":"2. Choose the folder to watch","url":"first-rule.html#2-choose-the-folder-to-watch","text":"In Watch, enter ~Downloads. The ~ form is a portable path that follows whichever Windows user is signed in. Other aliases are ~Documents, ~Desktop, ~Pictures, ~Music and ~Videos. Leave Include subfolders off. The first screenshot shows step 1 with the name and the watch folder filled in. The rule is not switched on yet, so the toolbar offers Start watching."},{"page":"Your first rule","section":"Getting started","heading":"3. Say which files (If)","url":"first-rule.html#3-say-which-files-if","text":"Press Next. In If, keep the group on All of the following and add two rows: File type Is PDF. File contents (text) Contains invoice. The line under the rows reports how many files in the folder match right now (here 9 of 17) and lists them, as the next screenshot shows. File contents reads the text inside PDFs, Word, Excel and PowerPoint files and plain text files. If some of your invoices are scans, open the gear at the end of the contents row, shown in the next screenshot. Pictures and scanned PDFs are read with OCR when Read text from images and scanned PDFs (OCR) is ticked, which is the default. OCR can misread, so Deckhand never deletes, recycles or overwrites a file because of text it recognised unless you approve that file. See the Conditions reference."},{"page":"Your first rule","section":"Getting started","heading":"4. Say what happens (Then)","url":"first-rule.html#4-say-what-happens-then","text":"Press Next and add a Move file action with this destination: ~Documents\\Invoices\\{modified:yyyy} {modified:yyyy} is a variable. It inserts the year the file was last modified, so each year gets its own folder. In the next screenshot it is the Date modified yyyy chip after ~Documents\\Invoices\\. See Variables and transforms. Leave If the file already exists (in the gear at the end of the row) on its default, Rename the new file. A clash then produces name_2.pdf instead of overwriting anything."},{"page":"Your first rule","section":"Getting started","heading":"5. Preview","url":"first-rule.html#5-preview","text":"Press Next, then Preview. Deckhand shows what would happen and changes nothing, as in the next screenshot. Changes lists files that would move, with the folder each one Becomes. Here 9 invoices go to Invoices\\2026 or Invoices\\2025, depending on the year each was modified. No match lists files the rule ignored (4 here, for example a PDF that is not an invoice). Problems lists files that would fail, with the reason. Untick any file you want left alone for this run. To try one file against the conditions, use Test one file.... You can also use Simulate. Read more in Preview, Run and Undo."},{"page":"Your first rule","section":"Getting started","heading":"6. Run, then undo if needed","url":"first-rule.html#6-run-then-undo-if-needed","text":"Press Run on 9 files in the toolbar. Deckhand asks you to confirm and says what it will do (here: Move: 9). It does exactly what you previewed. Open History. The run is listed with every file and step. Open the ... menu of the run and choose Undo this run to put the files back, or open the run's details to undo a single file. The next screenshot shows History filtered to this run, with that menu open. Deckhand asks once more before it undoes anything."},{"page":"Your first rule","section":"Getting started","heading":"7. Switch it on","url":"first-rule.html#7-switch-it-on","text":"When you are happy, save the rule and press Start watching. Deckhand handles the files already in the watch folder, then keeps watching. A file is only moved once it has stopped changing, so a download in progress is left alone. See Rules, folders and watching."},{"page":"Your first rule","section":"Getting started","heading":"Next","url":"first-rule.html#next","text":"Conditions and exclusions to keep files out of a rule. Actions and conflicts for every action and what happens when a name is taken."},{"page":"Rules, folders and watching","section":"Concepts","heading":"Rules, folders and watching","url":"rules-and-watching.html","text":"A rule watches one or more folders and handles the files that match its conditions. Switch it on and it handles the files already in its folders, then keeps watching. The Rules page lists your rules grouped by topic, in run order. Each row has its switch, the actions, the watched folders and a live status line. The screenshot shows the groups Downloads, Documents and the start of Media. The groups Development and Housekeeping follow further down. A rule that is switched off, such as Invoices to Documents, shows Stopped, and a rule that needs attention, such as Scans, has an amber status."},{"page":"Rules, folders and watching","section":"Concepts","heading":"What a rule contains","url":"rules-and-watching.html#what-a-rule-contains","text":"Part Question it answers **Watch** Which folders does it look at, and does it include subfolders? **If** Which files does it handle? See [Conditions and exclusions](conditions-and-exclusions.html). **Then** What happens to each matching file? See [Actions and conflicts](actions-and-conflicts.html). **Exclusions** Which files must it never touch? **Schedule** (optional) Should it also run at set times?"},{"page":"Rules, folders and watching","section":"Concepts","heading":"Three ways a rule looks at files","url":"rules-and-watching.html#three-ways-a-rule-looks-at-files","text":"Mode How it works Use it when **Live** Deckhand is told when files in the folder change and reacts. This is the default. New files should be handled soon after they arrive. **Rescan** Deckhand re-scans the folder every N minutes. The folder is on a network drive or another place where live notifications are unreliable. **Schedule** The rule runs at set times: daily, on chosen days, on a day of the month, or every few minutes or hours. You want a tidy-up at a fixed time. The rule Project backups: zip every Friday evening is scheduled and does not watch live. The screenshot shows its Schedule section: On chosen days at 18:00 with Fri selected, Run at start if a run was missed ticked and Also watch the folders live not ticked. A schedule can run with or without live watching. Run at start if a run was missed makes up one run that was due while Deckhand was closed. Deckhand has to be running, and running in the tray counts. The Schedules page lists everything that runs at a set time, presets and rules together, with the next and last run and a Run now button."},{"page":"Rules, folders and watching","section":"Concepts","heading":"Files are waited for","url":"rules-and-watching.html#files-are-waited-for","text":"Deckhand never moves a file that is still being copied or downloaded. A file waits until it has been unchanged for a number of seconds. The default is 15. You can change it in Settings, or per rule with the gear next to the watch folder. 0 means do not wait. Files with an unfinished extension such as .crdownload, .part or .tmp are left alone. Locked files and online-only cloud files are left alone too. Hidden and system files are skipped unless you allow them in Settings. A rule that is waiting says so in its status. It is not marked as needing attention. In a preview, such files are listed under Not ready."},{"page":"Rules, folders and watching","section":"Concepts","heading":"Rules run top to bottom","url":"rules-and-watching.html#rules-run-top-to-bottom","text":"Every enabled rule sees every file in its folders, in the order shown on the Rules page. The number in the Runs column is the position. A file moved by rule 1 is then seen by rule 2 at its new location. Each rule handles a file once per modified time, so a rule does not loop on a file it has already handled. There is no \"stop processing\" step. To keep two rules from handling the same file, make their conditions exclusive. To change the order, drag a row, press Alt+Up or Alt+Down, click the number and type a new one, or use Move to position in the ... menu. Switched-off rules keep their number."},{"page":"Rules, folders and watching","section":"Concepts","heading":"Folder options","url":"rules-and-watching.html#folder-options","text":"Include subfolders also watches folders inside the watch folder. Skip folders (the gear next to the watch folder): a bare name such as node_modules is skipped wherever it occurs, and a path is skipped with everything under it. Skipped folders are not scanned, so they cost no time. The screenshot shows the gear of the rule Dev: recycle stale build output opened. Under Skip these folders the names node_modules and .git are listed, and a box adds more. Ignore files already in the folders this rule moves to is on by default. It stops a rule looking again at files that already sit in its own destinations. If you drop a file straight into a destination folder, the rule will not touch it. Turn the option off if you want it to. Portable paths such as ~Downloads and ~Documents follow the current Windows user."},{"page":"Rules, folders and watching","section":"Concepts","heading":"Switching a rule off while it works","url":"rules-and-watching.html#switching-a-rule-off-while-it-works","text":"The switch follows your click at once. While Deckhand winds down you see Stopping..., then Stopped. A file that was stopped is shown as Stopped in History. It is not a failure, and nothing is left half done. The file is looked at again when you switch the rule back on."},{"page":"Conditions and exclusions","section":"Concepts","heading":"Conditions and exclusions","url":"conditions-and-exclusions.html","text":"The If section decides which files a rule handles. Each row tests one thing about the file. Exclusions are a separate safety net that wins over everything else."},{"page":"Conditions and exclusions","section":"Concepts","heading":"Rows and groups","url":"conditions-and-exclusions.html#rows-and-groups","text":"Rows are joined by a group: Group A file matches when **All of the following** Every row is true. **Any of the following** At least one row is true. **None of the following** No row is true. The first screenshot is the rule Installers: clear out after 14 days in the Full editor. An All of the following group holds three things: the file extension matches ^(exe|msi|msix)$, the date modified is older than 14 days, and a None of the following group that leaves out file names containing portable or keep. The list underneath shows the 9 files in Downloads that match. To nest a group, choose All / Any of the following in a row's first dropdown. The row turns into a group you can fill with its own rows. The second screenshot is the rule Scans: read the sender and name the file. An All of the following group contains the extension test, an Any of the following group with three File contents (text) rows (Amount due, Statement and a regular expression), and a file size test, larger than 100 Kb. A scan matches when it has one of those phrases. An empty group matches nothing, and so does \"none of\" an empty or unfinished group. Rows you have not finished (for example, no text typed yet) never match. This is on purpose, so a half-built rule cannot catch every file."},{"page":"Conditions and exclusions","section":"Concepts","heading":"What a row can test","url":"conditions-and-exclusions.html#what-a-row-can-test","text":"Names and paths: file name, extension, folder name, folder path. Dates: created, modified, accessed, compared with now (\"older than 30 days\") or a fixed day. Size: bytes, Kb, Mb or Gb. File type: a group such as Audio or Spreadsheet, or a specific type. Metadata: PDF, audio and image details such as author, artist or date taken. File contents: the text inside a file. One entry covers plain text, PDF, Word, Excel, PowerPoint and OpenDocument files and, through OCR, pictures and scanned PDFs. Its gear sets how the text is read. Duplicates and folder age. The full list is in the Conditions reference."},{"page":"Conditions and exclusions","section":"Concepts","heading":"Text tests and wildcards","url":"conditions-and-exclusions.html#text-tests-and-wildcards","text":"Text rows compare names, paths and other text. With wildcards on: Pattern Meaning `*` Any letters inside one word `**` Anything `?` One character A space Any whitespace \"Does not contain\" and other negative tests are true when the value is missing. For example, \"author is not Bob\" matches a file with no author. Positive tests, and number and date tests, are false when the value is missing."},{"page":"Conditions and exclusions","section":"Concepts","heading":"Duplicates","url":"conditions-and-exclusions.html#duplicates","text":"The duplicate condition matches copies of the same file. One copy is always kept, the oldest or the newest as you choose. The others match. Deckhand never removes the last copy."},{"page":"Conditions and exclusions","section":"Concepts","heading":"Exclusions","url":"conditions-and-exclusions.html#exclusions","text":"Excluded files are files a rule or preset never touches. Add them in the Exclusions section of the editor, or from the \"N files unticked\" bar under a preview. Exclude by Example This exact file A specific path File name `invoice.pdf` Name pattern `draft*` Folder `C:\\Scans\\Keep` Extension `.log` The next screenshot is the Exclusions dialog of the Installers rule. It lists three exclusions with their type, value, an optional note and the day each was added: the folder Downloads\\Keep, the name pattern setup-for-work* and one file. A new exclusion is added in the row at the bottom. Excluded files are listed under Excluded in the preview. They are never remembered as handled and are never changed. Remove an exclusion and the rule looks at the file again."},{"page":"Conditions and exclusions","section":"Concepts","heading":"Skipping folders in a watch folder","url":"conditions-and-exclusions.html#skipping-folders-in-a-watch-folder","text":"Use the gear next to the watch folder to skip whole folders. See Rules, folders and watching."},{"page":"Actions and conflicts","section":"Concepts","heading":"Actions and conflicts","url":"actions-and-conflicts.html","text":"Actions run top to bottom on each matching file. Each action has its own options, in the gear next to it. The first screenshot is the Then step of the rule Invoices to Documents. It has one action, Move file, with a destination made of text and the Date modified yyyy variable. The gear at the end of the row holds If the file already exists and the other options of that action."},{"page":"Actions and conflicts","section":"Concepts","heading":"The actions","url":"actions-and-conflicts.html#the-actions","text":"Action What it does **Move file** Moves the file to a folder, keeping its name. **Copy file** Copies it. Later actions can continue on the copy. **Rename file** Gives it a new name. The extension is kept. **Change extension** Changes the extension only. **Sort in folders** Puts the file in a subfolder by type, extension or date. **Zip / Extract** Adds the file to a zip, or unpacks an archive. **Send to Recycle Bin** Recycles the file. Windows can restore it. **Delete file** Deletes permanently. This cannot be undone. **Run command / Run saved script** Runs a program or script for each file. **Log / Notify** Writes a line to the log, or shows a message. **Remove folder if empty** Removes the file's folder when it has become empty. Details and options for each are in the Actions reference. The second screenshot is the end of the rule Release builds: sign, then publish to the NAS. It has two actions. Run saved script runs the script Sign release with the file path as its argument, then Copy file copies the signed installer to \\nas eleasesdeckhand."},{"page":"Actions and conflicts","section":"Concepts","heading":"When a file already exists","url":"actions-and-conflicts.html#when-a-file-already-exists","text":"Each move, copy, rename and similar action has an If the file already exists choice: Choice Result **Rename the new file** (default) The new file becomes `name_2`, `name_3` and so on. **Skip** The file is left where it is. **Overwrite the old file** The old file goes to the Recycle Bin, then the new one takes its place. The starting choice for new actions is in Settings → Behavior. Existing rules are not changed when you change it. Overwrite never recycles a folder."},{"page":"Actions and conflicts","section":"Concepts","heading":"If a value is missing","url":"actions-and-conflicts.html#if-a-value-is-missing","text":"When a {variable} in a name or folder has no value for a file, the action can: Skip the file: it is left alone and later actions continue. Use a fallback you typed. Mark the file as a problem: the file's remaining steps stop and it shows as skipped with the reason \"no value\". You can also put a fallback inside the text, for example {client|Unknown}."},{"page":"Actions and conflicts","section":"Concepts","heading":"Safety rules for steps","url":"actions-and-conflicts.html#safety-rules-for-steps","text":"If a step fails or is skipped, later steps that would delete or recycle the file do not run. A copy followed by a recycle can never lose the only copy. A failed step stops that file's remaining steps. Copy, zip, extract and run commands wait while the file is still being written. Extract never overwrites existing files. Run commands that take longer than 10 minutes are stopped."},{"page":"Actions and conflicts","section":"Concepts","heading":"Mirror subfolders","url":"actions-and-conflicts.html#mirror-subfolders","text":"With Include subfolders on, a Move, Copy or Zip action can keep the folder structure. C:\\In\\a\\b\\x.txt goes to D:\\Out\\a\\b\\x.txt instead of D:\\Out\\x.txt. The option is in the action's gear and shows an example. Undo removes folders the step created once they are empty."},{"page":"Actions and conflicts","section":"Concepts","heading":"Sort in folders","url":"actions-and-conflicts.html#sort-in-folders","text":"Sort creates subfolders under a base folder and puts each file in the right one: Sort by Folder name Type `Category\\Subtype`, for example `Microsoft Office\\Word` Extension `PDF` Year `2026` Month `2026-11` Day `2026-11-19` Extensions that are not in the file-type list go to Other\\EXT. Dates use the file's modified date."},{"page":"Actions and conflicts","section":"Concepts","heading":"Recycle or delete","url":"actions-and-conflicts.html#recycle-or-delete","text":"Send to Recycle Bin can be undone from Windows. Delete file cannot. When in doubt, recycle. Run a rule with Log first if you are unsure what it will catch."},{"page":"Presets","section":"Concepts","heading":"Presets","url":"presets.html","text":"A preset is a saved set of conditions and actions that is not tied to one folder. You pick a folder when you run it, preview what would happen, then run. The Presets page lists the built-in presets and your own, in groups such as Sort by type, Clean up and Development. Each row shows its actions and has a Run on a folder... button. The Schedules box at the top lists the presets that run by themselves, with the folder, the time, the next and last run and the status."},{"page":"Presets","section":"Concepts","heading":"Preset or rule?","url":"presets.html#preset-or-rule","text":"Rule Preset Folder Fixed watch folders Chosen each time you run it Runs All the time, or on a schedule When you start it, or on a schedule Use it for Keeping a folder tidy One-off or repeatable jobs on any folder Use Make a rule from this preset to turn a preset into a rule. A preset is edited with the same widgets as a rule, but it has no watch folder. The screenshot is the preset Screenshots by month: a name, a group and a description, the conditions (a file name that starts with Screenshot and the extension png) and a Move file action to a year and month folder made of the Date created yyyy and Date created MM variables. Include subfolders by default is not ticked."},{"page":"Presets","section":"Concepts","heading":"Run a preset on a folder","url":"presets.html#run-a-preset-on-a-folder","text":"The screenshot shows Screenshots by month run on PicturesScreenshots from the Home page. The preview is open: 14 changes ticked, each with the year and month folder it would move to, and 3 files with no match. Run on 14 files starts the run. Open Presets and choose Run on a folder. Choose the folder, and tick Include subfolders if you want them. Press Preview. You see the same preview as for a rule. Tick the files you want, then press Run. The Becomes column shows where each file will end up. Every run can be undone from the same window or from History. Nothing changes until you press Run. Each time you open Run on a folder, it starts fresh. Closing a preset that has preview results asks before it discards them. Preview work shows in Diagnostics with the Preview trigger. Only actions you actually run are recorded in History."},{"page":"Presets","section":"Concepts","heading":"Built-in presets","url":"presets.html#built-in-presets","text":"Built-in presets can be edited. Reset to original brings the original back."},{"page":"Presets","section":"Concepts","heading":"Scheduling a preset","url":"presets.html#scheduling-a-preset","text":"A preset can run on a schedule. Scheduled presets appear on the Schedules page alongside rules. See Rules, folders and watching."},{"page":"Presets","section":"Concepts","heading":"Sharing","url":"presets.html#sharing","text":"Presets can be exported and imported. Imported rules start disabled so you can review their paths and actions first. See Writing rules with an AI assistant for the import format."},{"page":"Guided and Full editor","section":"Guides","heading":"Guided and Full editor","url":"editors.html","text":"Deckhand has two views of the rule editor. Both edit the same rule, and you can switch at any time. Deckhand remembers your choice."},{"page":"Guided and Full editor","section":"Guides","heading":"Guided","url":"editors.html#guided","text":"Guided walks you through the rule one step at a time: Where and when (folders and schedule), Which files (If), What happens (Then) and Preview. It starts at the first step each time you open an editor. Use it for your first rules or when you want fewer things on screen. Your first rule goes through all four steps. The screenshot shows step 1 for the rule Invoices to Documents: the description (the rule's name), the watch folder ~Downloads and the schedule, which is not set. The step bar at the top, with Back and Next, is how you move between steps."},{"page":"Guided and Full editor","section":"Guides","heading":"Full editor","url":"editors.html#full-editor","text":"The Full editor shows every section at once: Watch, If, Then, Schedule, Preview and the rule options. Use it when you know what you are building or want to jump between sections. Press Full editor or Guided at the top of the page to switch. The screenshot is the rule Installers: clear out after 14 days in the Full editor. Description, Watch and If are on one page, with the list of files the conditions match right now (9 of 18) under the conditions. Then and Schedule follow below when you scroll."},{"page":"Guided and Full editor","section":"Guides","heading":"Juggle now","url":"editors.html#juggle-now","text":"Juggle now uses the same widgets as the rule editor for a job you do once. You pick the folders, say which files (If) and what to do (Then), then press Preview. Nothing is saved as a rule. Nothing changes until you press Run. The screenshot shows step 1 of Juggle now: the folder Projects, with Include subfolders ticked. The title says Nothing is saved or watched, and Save as rule... is in the toolbar. If the work turns out to be worth keeping, use Save as rule... or Save as preset.... Both ask for a name. If a rule (or preset) with that name already exists, the dialog says so and the button changes to Save anyway. Press it to save a second one with the same name, or change the name. Names are not unique in Deckhand, so give rules names you can tell apart. The command line and AI clients identify a rule by its id for that reason."},{"page":"Guided and Full editor","section":"Guides","heading":"Useful steps while editing","url":"editors.html#useful-steps-while-editing","text":"Watch: add the folder or folders. Use a portable path such as ~Downloads if the rule should follow the Windows user. Preview: see what would happen. Test one file... inside Preview picks a single file and shows exactly what the conditions and actions would do with it, without changing it. See Preview, Run and Undo. Save: saves the rule. The play switch is disabled while the rule has unsaved changes. If you rename a rule to a name another rule already has, Save warns once and then offers Save anyway. Switch on: starts watching (Start watching). A running rule is paused when you start editing it, and resumes when you save or cancel. Saving clears the rule's memory of which files it has already handled, so it looks at the folder again."},{"page":"Guided and Full editor","section":"Guides","heading":"Selecting several rules","url":"editors.html#selecting-several-rules","text":"On the Rules page, Ctrl+click toggles a rule, Shift+click selects a range, Ctrl+A selects everything shown. With two or more selected, a bar offers Enable, Disable, Duplicate, Move to group, Export and Delete. Delete asks first and lists the rules. Rows have no row of buttons. Click a row to open it. Use the ... button or right-click for everything else."},{"page":"Preview, Run and Undo","section":"Guides","heading":"Preview, Run and Undo","url":"preview-run-undo.html","text":"Preview makes no changes. Run does what you previewed. Undo reverses a run as far as it can. The first four screenshots follow one job in Juggle now: recycling stale build output (files under a arget folder, older than 21 days) in the Projects folder, and removing the folders that are left empty. The last two belong to the rule Invoices to Documents from Your first rule."},{"page":"Preview, Run and Undo","section":"Guides","heading":"Preview","url":"preview-run-undo.html#preview","text":"Press Preview in a rule, in Juggle now, or on Run on a folder for a preset. Deckhand shows what would happen and changes nothing. Tab Shows **Changes** Files that would change: file, what it **Becomes**, and size. **Unchanged** Files that are already fine. **Problems** Files that would fail. **Report by reason** groups them. **No match** Files the conditions did not select. **Excluded** Files your exclusions kept out. **Not ready** Files that are still changing, locked or unfinished. Preview uses the same code as a real run. The screenshot is the preview of the build clean-up job: 40 files in the Changes tab, each with a tick box, the action it would do under Becomes, and its size, plus a No match tab with 3 files. The button at the top says Run on 40 files, and the status bar says nothing has been touched yet."},{"page":"Preview, Run and Undo","section":"Guides","heading":"Tick what you want","url":"preview-run-undo.html#tick-what-you-want","text":"Untick any file you want left alone for this run. Press Exclude... to keep chosen files out for good. See Conditions and exclusions. Include files still changing acts on files that are still being written, for this run only. An unfinished .crdownload is still never touched."},{"page":"Preview, Run and Undo","section":"Guides","heading":"Right-click a file","url":"preview-run-undo.html#right-click-a-file","text":"View contents shows a file's text, plus details for PDF, Office, audio and pictures, without opening another program. Nothing is changed. Dragging a file out of a list into Explorer copies it there. The original stays."},{"page":"Preview, Run and Undo","section":"Guides","heading":"Stale previews","url":"preview-run-undo.html#stale-previews","text":"If you edit the rule after a preview, the preview is stale. Run is disabled until you preview again, so you never run something different from what you reviewed."},{"page":"Preview, Run and Undo","section":"Guides","heading":"Run","url":"preview-run-undo.html#run","text":"Press Run. Deckhand asks you to confirm and says what it will do, as in the next screenshot. Deckhand then re-checks each ticked file and does what the preview showed. The result card shows how many files are done (40 here) and offers Undo this run. Run can also be started with Run now on the Schedules page, or from the command line. See Command line."},{"page":"Preview, Run and Undo","section":"Guides","heading":"Undo","url":"preview-run-undo.html#undo","text":"Every run is recorded in History with each step. Undo this run puts files back where they were, as far as that is still possible. A file you changed since is left alone and reported. To undo one file, open the run's Details and press the undo icon on that file. Folders a Move or Copy created are removed again when they are empty. Can be undone Cannot be undone Move, copy, rename, sort, zip, extract, recycle Delete, and anything a run command or script did The last two screenshots show undoing the Invoices to Documents run from History. The first is History filtered to that run, with the ... menu of its row open and Undo this run in it. The second is the question Deckhand asks before it moves the files back. Recycled files come back from the Recycle Bin. Overwritten files are in the Recycle Bin too. Permanent delete has no undo. Prefer Send to Recycle Bin, and preview first."},{"page":"Preview, Run and Undo","section":"Guides","heading":"Problems and left-alone files","url":"preview-run-undo.html#problems-and-left-alone-files","text":"The Problems tab lists files that failed (red) or were left alone on purpose (amber), each with the reason. Export CSV saves the list."},{"page":"Simulate","section":"Guides","heading":"Simulate","url":"simulate.html","text":"Simulate answers \"what would my rules do to a file like this?\" It follows an example file through your rules, in order, and shows what each rule decides. Simulate itself never changes a real file."},{"page":"Simulate","section":"Guides","heading":"Use a made-up file when rules are switched on","url":"simulate.html#use-a-made-up-file-when-rules-are-switched-on","text":"Simulate does not stop your live rules. If you point it at a real file that sits in a folder a rule is watching, and that rule is switched on, the rule may move, rename or delete the file while you look at it, and the result you read will not match what you see on disk. Rules are on and watching: use a made-up path such as C:\\Users\\You\\Downloads\\Steam.exe. It does not have to exist. Want to test with a real file: switch the rules off first, or copy the file to a folder no rule watches, and turn rules on inside Simulate only. Changes there stay in the simulation."},{"page":"Simulate","section":"Guides","heading":"What a made-up file cannot show","url":"simulate.html#what-a-made-up-file-cannot-show","text":"A path that does not exist has no contents and no embedded details, so Simulate cannot tell what depends on them: file contents, checksums, and PDF, audio or image details. It reports those as \"needs more information\" instead of guessing. Use a real file for those, with the live rules off."},{"page":"Simulate","section":"Guides","heading":"Try a file name","url":"simulate.html#try-a-file-name","text":"Open Simulate. Enter the path of an example file. It does not have to exist. You can also give a size and created and modified dates. If the file does exist, you can use the real file's facts. Read the report: each rule, whether the file matched, and where the file ends up after each step. The first screenshot uses the made-up path C:\\Users\\ScriptKiddyMonkey\\Downloads\\VSCodeSetup-x64-1.9.exe. The left side lists the rules in run order with the ones that are ticked. Switched-off rules are struck through and have no number. On the right, What would happen follows the file down the list: rule 1, Installers: clear out after 14 days, is marked Matched, shows each of its conditions with the result, and ends with the action it would run (Recycle, send to the Recycle Bin). Rule 2 and the later rules show No match. A different name matches a different rule. In the second screenshot the path is C:\\Users\\ScriptKiddyMonkey\\Pictures\\Screenshots\\NewScreenshot 2026-11-19 093412.png. Rule 1 now says No match, because the extension is not exe, msi or msix. Rule 7, Screenshots: file by date, is Matched. Because rules run top to bottom and a later rule sees the file where an earlier one left it, Simulate is the quickest way to find rules that fight over the same files."},{"page":"Simulate","section":"Guides","heading":"Edit without risk","url":"simulate.html#edit-without-risk","text":"Edits, ticks and ordering in Simulate belong to the simulation only. Reorder rules, switch some off, or change a condition to see the effect. Your saved rules are not changed."},{"page":"Simulate","section":"Guides","heading":"Simulate, Test one file or Preview?","url":"simulate.html#simulate-test-one-file-or-preview","text":"Use To **Test one file...** (in a rule's Preview) Pick one real file and see what one rule's conditions and actions would do with it. **Simulate** See what a set of rules does to a file when they run in order. **Preview** See what a rule will really do to the files in its folder. Simulate stands in for a real folder. When you need to know what will happen to actual files, use Preview."},{"page":"History and the Log","section":"Guides","heading":"History and the Log","url":"history-and-log.html","text":"History has two views of the same activity: Runs and the Log."},{"page":"History and the Log","section":"Guides","heading":"Runs","url":"history-and-log.html#runs","text":"Every run is listed with its result. The screenshot shows the Runs tab with a search box and filters on top, then one row per run: when it started, the rule, its folders and the result, with Details and a ... menu on each row. Open a run to see Details: every file and step. You can undo one file from here. The screenshot is the details of the big run of Screenshots: file by date on 18 November: 1284 files done, each with its action (Move), the result and where it Becomes. The undo icon at the end of a row undoes that one file. The ... menu on a run has Undo this run and Remove. The Problems tab shows failed and left-alone files with the reason, and can export them to CSV. Runs from rules and presets, and runs started from the command line, appear here. The command line shows as the source cli."},{"page":"History and the Log","section":"Guides","heading":"Log","url":"history-and-log.html#log","text":"The Log lists every action on every file. Search it, and filter by result, rule, date and kind of work (moves, copies, renames, deletes, commands and so on). Large runs are searched by the backend, so the filter covers all files, not only those on screen. In the screenshot the Log tab lists the latest actions, one line per file: when, the result, the rule, the file, the action, the result details and where the file becomes. Lines of the rule Scans carry an OCR mark, because their text was read with OCR. The filters above the list narrow it by result, rule, kind of work and dates."},{"page":"History and the Log","section":"Guides","heading":"Home counters","url":"history-and-log.html#home-counters","text":"The Home page counts files processed and data handled. Only files an action really changed count: files that were already fine, skipped, excluded, failed or undone do not. The counters come from your History. The menu on the counters resets or restores them. Your files, History and Undo are not touched."},{"page":"History and the Log","section":"Guides","heading":"History size","url":"history-and-log.html#history-size","text":"Settings controls how much history and log Deckhand keeps. History and logs can contain file names, paths, destinations and error messages. See the privacy policy."},{"page":"Diagnostics","section":"Guides","heading":"Diagnostics","url":"diagnostics.html","text":"Diagnostics shows what Deckhand costs your PC and how long each rule or preset takes. Use it to find a rule that is slower than it should be. The top of the page has two tiles: the memory and CPU of Deckhand as a whole, with a small chart, and how much work Deckhand did this session. Below them, every rule and preset has a row with the columns described further down."},{"page":"Diagnostics","section":"Guides","heading":"Whole-app CPU and memory","url":"diagnostics.html#whole-app-cpu-and-memory","text":"At the top you see one memory figure and one CPU figure for Deckhand as a whole. Memory is the private working set, as in Task Manager's Processes tab. CPU is a share of all logical processors, as in Task Manager's Processes tab. A small chart shows the last few minutes. The numbers refresh about every two seconds while the page is visible. Hover the figures for a split between the Deckhand engine and the windows that draw the interface. Memory is shown for the whole app only, not per rule. The rule list does not have a memory column, because rules share the same engine."},{"page":"Diagnostics","section":"Guides","heading":"Per rule and per preset","url":"diagnostics.html#per-rule-and-per-preset","text":"Each rule and preset has a row with these columns: Column Meaning **Runs** How many times it ran this session. **Average** Average duration of a run. **CPU time** CPU time of the working thread, for example 1.2 s. This is time spent computing, not a percentage. **Files** Files looked at. **Matched** Files that matched the conditions. **Actions** Actions completed. Click a column title to sort. Filter by name or by status. Expand a row for the details of each run and the totals: total time, average run, slowest run, CPU time in total. The next screenshot has the row Screenshots: file by date expanded. Under the status line are eight totals (runs this session, total time, average run, slowest run, CPU time in total, files looked at, matched and actions done), then the Recent runs table with the time, trigger, duration, CPU time, files, matched and actions of each run. A Slowest runs tab sorts the same list by duration."},{"page":"Diagnostics","section":"Guides","heading":"Triggers","url":"diagnostics.html#triggers","text":"Each run records why it started: Trigger Meaning **initial scan** The first scan of the watched folders after the rule starts. **rescan** A later scan (interval or recheck). **preview** A rule or preset preview that checked files without running actions. **change event** A file appeared or changed in a watched folder. **retry** A file that had to wait and was looked at again. **schedule** A scheduled run. **manual** Started by hand (Run, Run once, Run now)."},{"page":"Diagnostics","section":"Guides","heading":"Keeping the cost low","url":"diagnostics.html#keeping-the-cost-low","text":"The heaviest work is the first look through a big folder, reading PDF and Office text, and OCR. Settings → Performance: engine priority Low (the default) keeps your PC responsive. Normal gives rules the same priority as your programs. Pause rules on battery makes rules wait until the PC is plugged in. Skip folders you do not need (the gear next to a watch folder). Skipped folders are never scanned. Untick Read text from images and scanned PDFs (OCR) in the gear of File contents conditions that do not need it."},{"page":"Conditions reference","section":"Reference","heading":"Conditions reference","url":"conditions-reference.html","text":"This page lists what the If section can test. Names in the app may be worded a little differently. The names in code are the ones used in the draft JSON format."},{"page":"Conditions reference","section":"Reference","heading":"Groups","url":"conditions-reference.html#groups","text":"Condition Meaning `all` Every item must match (AND). An empty group never matches. `any` At least one item must match (OR). An empty group never matches. `not` The item must not match. Not of an empty or unfinished item is false. `all_files` Matches every file. Groups nest freely."},{"page":"Conditions reference","section":"Reference","heading":"Text","url":"conditions-reference.html#text","text":"Compares a text value such as the name, extension, path, folder name or file contents. Operator Meaning `is`, `is_not` Equals, does not equal `contains`, `not_contains` Contains, does not contain `starts_with`, `ends_with` Begins or ends with `regex`, `not_regex` Matches or does not match a regular expression `empty`, `not_empty` Has no value, or has a value Case-insensitive unless you choose case-sensitive. Wildcards (when on): * stays within a word, ** spans anything, ? is one character, a space is any whitespace. The value can hold {variable} tokens, and you can compare with another variable. Regular expressions are .NET-style: lookaround, backreferences and named groups work. The extension is written without the dot. Negative operators are true when the value is missing. Positive operators are false. The next screenshot is the rule Receipts: file by year and month. Its second row is a File contents (text) condition that matches the regular expression \\b(receipt|order total|amount paid)\\b. The magnifier button at the end of such a row opens the expression tester. In the tester, Open preview file... loads a real file, here a receipt PDF. Every match in its text is highlighted and the extracted value is shown under the text. Press Use these settings to keep the expression."},{"page":"Conditions reference","section":"Reference","heading":"Number","url":"conditions-reference.html#number","text":"For size and numeric metadata. Operator Meaning `gt`, `ge`, `lt`, `le` Greater than, at least, less than, at most `eq`, `ne` Equal, not equal `between` Between two values Size takes bytes, kb, mb or gb, where 1 kb is 1024 bytes. A blank or non-numeric value never matches."},{"page":"Conditions reference","section":"Reference","heading":"Date","url":"conditions-reference.html#date","text":"Operator Meaning `older_than`, `newer_than` Compared with now, using an amount and a unit: minutes, hours, days, weeks, months or years `before`, `after`, `on` Compared with a fixed date, `YYYY-MM-DD`, optionally with `HH:MM` Dates you can test include created, modified, accessed and, for pictures, the date taken. Empty or unfinished rows never match."},{"page":"Conditions reference","section":"Reference","heading":"File type","url":"conditions-reference.html#file-type","text":"Matches by file type. Use a group name such as image, video, audio, archive, pdf, document, spreadsheet, presentation, program, code, font, script, installer, diskimage, cad3d, data, database, webpage, email or temp. You can also give a specific Category\\Subtype, such as Microsoft Office\\Word. A group matches every extension in it, so you do not list them one by one. Negate it to match everything else."},{"page":"Conditions reference","section":"Reference","heading":"Duplicate","url":"conditions-reference.html#duplicate","text":"Matches a copy of a file that is not the one being kept. Duplicates are files with the same size and SHA-256. You choose which folders to compare (the watched folder by default), whether to include subfolders, and whether to keep the oldest or the newest. One copy always stays. Empty files are never duplicates."},{"page":"Conditions reference","section":"Reference","heading":"Folder age","url":"conditions-reference.html#folder-age","text":"Compares the age of the newest file in the file's own folder, with older_than or newer_than, an amount and a unit. Optionally includes subfolders. Useful for cleaning up folders nobody has touched for months."},{"page":"Conditions reference","section":"Reference","heading":"Metadata","url":"conditions-reference.html#metadata","text":"Use the variables in Variables and transforms: PDF fields, audio tags and picture details. PDF, audio and picture fields are empty for other kinds of file."},{"page":"Conditions reference","section":"Reference","heading":"File contents","url":"conditions-reference.html#file-contents","text":"There is one File contents (text) entry in the variable list (contents in a draft). It reads the text of any supported file: plain text files, PDF, Word, Excel, PowerPoint and OpenDocument files, which have text of their own; pictures and scanned PDFs, through Windows OCR. The gear at the end of the row holds the options for that one condition, as the next screenshot shows for the rule Scans: read the sender and name the file: Option What it does **Read text from images and scanned PDFs (OCR)** On by default. OCR is used only when a file has no text of its own. Matches found this way are marked in the preview, and a file is never deleted, recycled or overwritten because of recognised text unless you approve that file. **Read PDF in columns (column by column)** Reads a PDF with several columns one column after the other, instead of line by line across the page. **Show what Deckhand reads from a file...** Opens a window where you pick a file and see the exact text Deckhand reads from it with these options. In the screenshot the gear of the second File contents row of the Scans rule is open. The window behind Show what Deckhand reads from a file... has its own OCR and PDF in columns ticks, so you can compare the options on one file. Here it shows a scanned bank statement. Because the text came from OCR, the line under the file name says so and warns that it can contain mistakes. In a draft, the options are a contents object on the condition: { \"ocr\": true, \"columns\": false }. A default is left out. The old contents.columns variable is no longer in the list, but rules and drafts that use it still work: it is read as File contents with Read PDF in columns on. OCR is no longer a switch in Settings or on the rule. Each File contents condition has its own OCR option, and Settings keeps only the OCR language and a button that clears the OCR cache. See Settings."},{"page":"Conditions reference","section":"Reference","heading":"Wait","url":"conditions-reference.html#wait","text":"sleep waits up to 300 000 milliseconds, but only in real runs, not in previews."},{"page":"Actions reference","section":"Reference","heading":"Actions reference","url":"actions-reference.html","text":"Actions run in order for each matching file. Names in code are the ones used in the draft JSON format. The screenshot shows the two actions of the rule Release builds: Run saved script (run_script) with the script Sign release, then Copy file (copy) to a network folder."},{"page":"Actions reference","section":"Reference","heading":"Files and folders","url":"actions-reference.html#files-and-folders","text":"Action Options Undo `move` Destination (created if needed; relative means relative to the file's folder), if the name is taken, mirror subfolders, remove empty folders, if a value is missing Yes `copy` Same as move, plus **later actions use the copy** Yes `rename` New name without the extension, if taken, if a value is missing Yes `change_ext` New extension Yes (as a rename) `sort` Base folder (empty means the file's own folder), sort by type, extension, year, month or day Yes `recycle` Remove empty folders Yes, from the Recycle Bin `delete` Remove empty folders. **Permanent.** No `remove_empty_folder` Which folder (where the file started, or where it is now), and also empty parents Yes Move and copy keep the file name. Sort uses the file's modified date for year, month and day."},{"page":"Actions reference","section":"Reference","heading":"Archives","url":"actions-reference.html#archives","text":"Action Options Notes `extract` Destination (empty means a new folder next to the archive), delete the archive afterwards Handles zip, 7z, rar, tar and tar.gz. Never overwrites. `zip` Zip file, if taken, keep structure Adds the file to a .zip. The original stays. Undo reverses zip and extract. A zip never adds itself."},{"page":"Actions reference","section":"Reference","heading":"Commands and messages","url":"actions-reference.html#commands-and-messages","text":"Action Options Notes `run` Command, shell (default, cmd, PowerShell, pwsh), run elevated, time-out Stdout and stderr appear in History. Exit code 0 means done. Time-out default is 10 minutes. `run_script` A saved script and its arguments Scripts are managed on the **Scripts** page. `log` Message Writes to the log. Good for dry-running an idea. `notify` Message Shows a message. A saved script has a shell, a body, arguments and a time-out. The screenshot is the script Sign release that the rule above uses: a PowerShell script that signs the installer and writes its checksum, run with the file path as its argument and stopped after 300 seconds. Values from {variables} are made safe before they are inserted into a command. Run elevated needs Deckhand to be started as administrator. It never prompts. Undo does not cover what a command or script did."},{"page":"Actions reference","section":"Reference","heading":"Name conflicts","url":"actions-reference.html#name-conflicts","text":"Setting Choices If the name is taken (`on_conflict`) `rename` (adds `_2`, `_3`), `skip`, `overwrite` (the old file goes to the Recycle Bin) If a value is missing (`on_missing`) `fail`, `skip`, `fallback` See Actions and conflicts for the safety rules about skipped steps."},{"page":"Variables and transforms","section":"Reference","heading":"Variables and transforms","url":"variables-and-transforms.html","text":"Put {name} in a folder, a file name, a message or a command to use something read from the file. For example {year} or {filename}. The { } button in a text box lists what is available. The next screenshot is the Then section of the rule Scans: read the sender and name the file. The Rename file action builds the new name from three variable chips, Document date yyyy-MM-dd, Sender and File name slug. The Move file action puts the file in a folder named after the Sender."},{"page":"Variables and transforms","section":"Reference","heading":"Template syntax","url":"variables-and-transforms.html#template-syntax","text":"Write Result `{modified:yyyy-MM}` The value with a format, here `2026-11` `{client|Unknown}` The value, or `Unknown` when there is none `{image.taken:yyyy|@modified:yyyy}` The date taken, otherwise the modified year. A fallback starting with `@` names another variable. `{modified+7d:yyyy-MM}` Date math: add or subtract N minutes, hours, days, weeks, months or years (`m h d w mo y`) `{client>upper}` A transform. Chain them: `{client>trim>upper}` `{{` and `}}` A literal brace Order inside a token is: name, date math, :format, |fallbacks, then >transforms. Put the format on every part of a fallback chain. An unknown {name} is an error."},{"page":"Variables and transforms","section":"Reference","heading":"Date formats","url":"variables-and-transforms.html#date-formats","text":"Date formats use .NET patterns: yyyy, yy, MMMM, MMM, MM, dddd, ddd, dd, HH, hh, mm, ss, tt, and literal text in single quotes."},{"page":"Variables and transforms","section":"Reference","heading":"Built-in variables","url":"variables-and-transforms.html#built-in-variables","text":"Group Variables Name and place `name` (without extension), `ext`, `filename`, `path`, `folder_name`, `folder_path` Size and type `size` (bytes), `type`, `type.category`, `type.subtype`, `type.group` Dates `created`, `modified`, `accessed`, `now` Contents `contents` (File contents: text of any supported file, OCR for pictures and scans) Hashes `hash.md5`, `hash.sha1`, `hash.sha256` PDF `pdf.title`, `pdf.subject`, `pdf.keywords`, `pdf.author`, `pdf.creator`, `pdf.producer`, `pdf.created`, `pdf.modified` Audio `audio.artist`, `audio.album`, `audio.album_artist`, `audio.title`, `audio.genre`, `audio.track`, `audio.disc`, `audio.year`, `audio.duration` Pictures `image.make`, `image.model`, `image.camera`, `image.width`, `image.height`, `image.megapixels`, `image.taken`, `image.latitude`, `image.longitude` PDF, audio and picture variables are empty for other kinds of file. There is one contents variable. How it reads a file (OCR, PDF in columns) is set in the gear of the condition or variable that uses it. See File contents. The old contents.columns variable is gone from the list, and older rules that use it keep working."},{"page":"Variables and transforms","section":"Reference","heading":"Your own variables","url":"variables-and-transforms.html#your-own-variables","text":"The Variables page lets you define values pulled from a file's text or from another variable. The screenshot shows six variables of five kinds: a dictionary (Sender), a date (Document date), two regular expressions (Reference number, Save slot), a custom date (Backup stamp) and a word list (Project name). Kind What it finds Word list The first listed word found (wildcards allowed) Dictionary A result for the first search text found, for example \"payroll\" gives \"HR\" Regular expression The first match, or its first capture group Pattern A match built from parts such as digits, words and literal text, for example `Invoice No:` followed by digits Date The first date in the text, with a day-month-year order and output format Custom date The file's created, modified or accessed date, or now, with a format you choose Open a variable to edit it. The next screenshot is the dictionary variable Sender. Its Find column holds the text to look for in the file (for example BRIGHTSIDE ENERGY) and its Return column the clean name to use. Look in says where to search. File contents (text) is the default, and then the gear next to it opens the same reading options as a condition. The gear next to Look in sets how the file's text is read for this variable: Read text from images and scanned PDFs (OCR) (on by default), Read PDF in columns (column by column), and Show what Deckhand reads from a file.... The gear only appears while Look in is File contents. Pick from a sample helps with a regular expression: choose a file, select the value with the mouse and press Use selection. Deckhand builds the expression from the words in front of it. Add more samples to check that the expression finds the right thing in each."},{"page":"Variables and transforms","section":"Reference","heading":"Transforms","url":"variables-and-transforms.html#transforms","text":"Transform Effect `upper`, `lower`, `title` Change case `trim`, `collapse` Remove outer spaces, or squeeze repeated spaces `replace:find:with` Replace text `regex:pattern:replacement` Regular-expression replace, with `$1` for groups `substr:start:length`, `left:n`, `right:n` Take part of the text `pad:width[:char]`, `padright:width[:char]` Pad to a width `slug`, `sanitize` Make URL-friendly text, or remove characters not allowed in file names `digits`, `word:n` Keep digits only, or the Nth word `remove:text`, `prefix:text`, `suffix:text` Remove, add before, add after Escape a colon, a > or a backslash inside a transform argument with a backslash. Transforms also apply to the fallback: {client|UNKNOWN>upper}."},{"page":"Variables and transforms","section":"Reference","heading":"When a value is missing","url":"variables-and-transforms.html#when-a-value-is-missing","text":"If a variable has no value and no fallback, the action can skip the file, use a fallback, or mark the file as a problem. See Actions and conflicts."},{"page":"Settings","section":"Reference","heading":"Settings","url":"settings.html","text":"Settings holds defaults that apply to every rule. A setting that belongs to one rule, such as how a File contents condition reads pictures, lives in that rule instead. Changes are saved as you make them."},{"page":"Settings","section":"Reference","heading":"Look and editing","url":"settings.html#look-and-editing","text":"The first screenshot shows the top of the page. Appearance has the theme (Dark, Light or Follow Windows), the density of lists (Compact or Comfortable) and the Nautical skin. Editing rules has two switches: whether the + button copies the row above, and whether folders you choose are saved as portable paths such as ~Downloads so the rule works on any PC. Safety has three switches: leave read-only and system files alone, include hidden files and folders, and ask before every run."},{"page":"Settings","section":"Reference","heading":"Waiting, defaults and notifications","url":"settings.html#waiting-defaults-and-notifications","text":"The next screenshot continues down the page. Files still being written sets how long a file must stay unchanged before a rule touches it (5 seconds in the screenshot), the unfinished file extensions Deckhand skips, and whether online-only cloud files may be downloaded. See Rules, folders and watching. Behavior sets the starting choice of When a file already exists for new move, copy, rename and zip actions. Existing rules are not changed. See Actions and conflicts. Notifications controls Windows notifications, how often Deckhand tells you that a rule needs attention, the optional summary after files are handled, quiet hours, and whether clicking a notification opens the rule. Performance is the card at the bottom of the screenshot."},{"page":"Settings","section":"Reference","heading":"Performance and running in the background","url":"settings.html#performance-and-running-in-the-background","text":"Engine priority is Low by default, so rules work quietly in the background. Normal gives rules the same priority as your programs. Pause rules while the PC runs on battery makes rules wait until the PC is plugged in. Running in the background has Start with Windows, Allow multiple Deckhand instances and what happens when you close the window while rules are running. See Diagnostics."},{"page":"Settings","section":"Reference","heading":"Text in pictures and scans (OCR)","url":"settings.html#text-in-pictures-and-scans-ocr","text":"This card has only two settings: Language: the OCR language. Empty means the languages of your Windows profile. The list shows the OCR languages installed in Windows. OCR cache: Clear OCR cache forgets the text Deckhand already read from pictures and scans. There is no switch here that turns OCR on or off. Whether a condition reads pictures and scanned PDFs is chosen in the gear of that File contents condition or variable. See File contents. If no OCR language is installed in Windows, the card says so, and the condition's gear explains that OCR is not available. See Troubleshooting."},{"page":"Settings","section":"Reference","heading":"Backup, AI integrations, command line and support","url":"settings.html#backup-ai-integrations-command-line-and-support","text":"Backup exports your rules, presets, variables, groups, schedules, settings, scripts and custom file types as one ZIP, and restores from such a ZIP (a safety copy of the current state is made first). AI integrations opens the setup for an AI client and the AI reference, see Connect an AI client (MCP). Command line shows the options and exit codes of your version, see Command line. Support Deckhand has Open the docs, which opens this documentation in your browser, Report a bug and Donate on Ko-fi."},{"page":"Settings","section":"Reference","heading":"Also in the settings","url":"settings.html#also-in-the-settings","text":"History and log sets how long history is kept and whether a detailed log is written. Your data shows where the library is kept. Windows Explorer menu adds an optional right-click entry for folders and needs no administrator rights."},{"page":"Settings","section":"Reference","heading":"Opening this documentation from the app","url":"settings.html#opening-this-documentation-from-the-app","text":"The online documentation is one click away from three places: the book button in the title bar (the first of the three buttons left of the window buttons), Open the docs under Support Deckhand in Settings, and Online docs at the top of the Help page. All three open this site in your browser and need an internet connection. The Help page itself has short answers and works without a network. The title bar of Deckhand, with the Online documentation button first of the three buttons before minimise, maximise and close: The Help page, with its search box and the three buttons at the top:"},{"page":"Keyboard shortcuts","section":"Reference","heading":"Keyboard shortcuts","url":"keyboard-shortcuts.html","text":"Deckhand is built to be used from the keyboard. Ctrl+K opens the command palette. It searches pages, actions, rules and your files. In the screenshot the word rule is typed, and the palette lists Go to Rules (with its shortcut Ctrl+3), New rule and Open rule entries for the saved rules."},{"page":"Keyboard shortcuts","section":"Reference","heading":"Everywhere","url":"keyboard-shortcuts.html#everywhere","text":"Shortcut Action [[Ctrl+1]] to [[Ctrl+9]] Open the first nine pages in sidebar order. Help and Settings have no number. [[Ctrl+K]] Open the command palette. It also searches your files. [[Esc]] Close the top menu or window first, then go back. [[Tab]] and [[Shift+Tab]] Move between controls. They stay inside an open window."},{"page":"Keyboard shortcuts","section":"Reference","heading":"In the rule editor","url":"keyboard-shortcuts.html#in-the-rule-editor","text":"Shortcut Action [[Ctrl+Enter]] Preview [[Ctrl+S]] Save"},{"page":"Keyboard shortcuts","section":"Reference","heading":"In lists","url":"keyboard-shortcuts.html#in-lists","text":"Shortcut Action [[Tab]] Reach the list [[Up]] and [[Down]], [[Home]], [[End]] Move between items [[Enter]] or [[Space]] Open the focused item [[Shift+F10]] or the context-menu key Open the row menu"},{"page":"Keyboard shortcuts","section":"Reference","heading":"On the Rules page","url":"keyboard-shortcuts.html#on-the-rules-page","text":"Shortcut Action [[Ctrl+A]] Select every shown rule [[Ctrl+Space]] Toggle one rule [[Shift+Up]] and [[Shift+Down]] Extend the selection [[Alt+Up]] and [[Alt+Down]] Move the focused rule one place in the run order **Ctrl+click**, **Shift+click** Toggle a rule, or select a range"},{"page":"Command line","section":"Reference","heading":"Command line","url":"command-line.html","text":"Deckhand can run a preset or rule without opening its window. Runs started this way appear in History with the source cli. The Command line card in Settings (screenshot) shows the usage and the exit codes of the version you have installed. The complete list of options and exit codes is shown in the app under Settings → Command line, which always matches the version you have installed."},{"page":"Command line","section":"Reference","heading":"Run a rule or a preset","url":"command-line.html#run-a-rule-or-a-preset","text":"Command What it does `Deckhand.exe --run-rule \"<name or id>\"` Runs a saved rule once on its own watch folders. It does not need to be switched on. `Deckhand.exe --run-preset \"<name or id>\" --folder \"<path>\"` Runs a preset on one folder. Without --yes (or with --dry-run) only the preview is printed and nothing is changed. Add --yes for a real run and --json for output a program can read. Prefer the id. Names are not unique: two rules can share one name. A name can be used when it is unique, but an id always finds exactly one rule or preset. A script or an AI client should use the id, which --list-presets --json and the MCP rule list return."},{"page":"Command line","section":"Reference","heading":"Exit codes","url":"command-line.html#exit-codes","text":"Code Meaning 0 Everything went well, or the preview found nothing that would fail. 1 The run finished but some steps failed, or the preview says some would fail. 2 Usage error: unknown option, missing value or missing `--folder`. Nothing was done. 3 Not found: no such preset, rule or folder. 4 A real run was asked for without `--yes`. Nothing was changed; the preview was printed. 5 Internal error, for example the run finished but could not be written to the history. 6 Ambiguous: several rules or presets have that name. Nothing was done. Run it again with the id. With --json, exit code 6 prints an error object with \"ambiguous\": true and a candidates list. Each candidate has its id, name, group and more, so a script can pick the right one and run the same command with that id: {\"schema\":1,\"mode\":\"error\",\"exit_code\":6,\"error\":\"...\",\"ambiguous\":true,\"candidates\":[{\"id\":\"r_inv\",\"name\":\"Invoices to Documents\",\"group\":\"Documents\",...}]}"},{"page":"Command line","section":"Reference","heading":"Where to find the options","url":"command-line.html#where-to-find-the-options","text":"Open Deckhand and go to Settings. Find the Command line card. It lists every option and exit code for your version."},{"page":"Command line","section":"Reference","heading":"Authoring helpers","url":"command-line.html#authoring-helpers","text":"These options help an AI tool or script that writes rule drafts. They validate and print information. They never import or run anything. Command What it does `Deckhand.exe --authoring-schema` Prints the JSON schema for rule drafts. `Deckhand.exe --authoring-catalogue` Lists IDs you can reuse in a draft. `Deckhand.exe --validate-draft draft.json` Checks a draft and reports structured errors. `Deckhand.exe --validate-draft draft.json --example \"C:\\full\\file.ext\"` Also simulates the draft against a synthetic example file. .\\Deckhand.exe --validate-draft .\\draft.json The schema is also published as deckhand-draft.schema.json."},{"page":"Command line","section":"Reference","heading":"Explorer menu","url":"command-line.html#explorer-menu","text":"Settings can add an optional Explorer right-click menu for Deckhand."},{"page":"Writing rules with an AI assistant","section":"AI and automation","heading":"Writing rules with an AI assistant","url":"ai-authoring.html","text":"An AI assistant can draft rules for you. It writes a JSON file. You import it, Deckhand checks it, and you review it before anything runs. The assistant never needs access to your files. Help → Author rules with AI... and Help → Connect an AI client... are on the Help page, and the same setup is in the AI integrations card of Settings, as in the screenshot."},{"page":"Writing rules with an AI assistant","section":"AI and automation","heading":"How it works","url":"ai-authoring.html#how-it-works","text":"In Deckhand, open Help → Author rules with AI.... It copies the reference material for your chosen AI tool. Describe what you want. A good assistant asks for the full Windows paths it needs before drafting, such as C:\\Finance\\Drop or a network path like \\\\server\\share\\Invoices. Save the JSON the assistant gives you to a file. In Deckhand, open Rules → Import and select the file. Deckhand validates it before showing Import. If there are errors, use Copy issues, give them to the assistant, and import the revised draft. Drafts are also refused when a name is over 200 characters, a path over 1000, an absolute destination contains .., or a {token} is not a known variable. Review the imported rule. Rules arrive disabled. Preview on a real folder before you switch one on. Tell the assistant to use log-only actions first for anything risky, and to prefer Recycle over Delete."},{"page":"Writing rules with an AI assistant","section":"AI and automation","heading":"What the assistant needs","url":"ai-authoring.html#what-the-assistant-needs","text":"Give your assistant these references: The authoring guide, a compact description of every field a draft may use. Worked examples, complete drafts that pass validation. The draft JSON schema."},{"page":"Writing rules with an AI assistant","section":"AI and automation","heading":"The shape of a draft","url":"ai-authoring.html#the-shape-of-a-draft","text":"A draft is a JSON object with deckhand_export set to 1 and optional lists of rules, presets, variables and scripts. A minimal example: { \"deckhand_export\": 1, \"rules\": [ { \"id\": \"pdfs\", \"name\": \"PDFs from Downloads\", \"enabled\": false, \"folders\": [{ \"path\": \"C:\\\\Users\\\\Me\\\\Downloads\" }], \"condition\": { \"kind\": \"file_type\", \"types\": [\"pdf\"] }, \"actions\": [ { \"kind\": \"move\", \"dest\": \"D:\\\\Documents\\\\PDFs\", \"on_conflict\": \"skip\" } ] } ] } Paths in JSON need doubled backslashes. Names of conditions and actions are listed in the Conditions reference and Actions reference."},{"page":"Writing rules with an AI assistant","section":"AI and automation","heading":"Reading the text of files","url":"ai-authoring.html#reading-the-text-of-files","text":"For tests on what is written inside a file, use one File contents condition (\"var\": \"contents\"). It reads text files, PDF, Word, Excel, PowerPoint and OpenDocument files, and pictures and scanned PDFs through Windows OCR. Its options go on the condition as \"contents\": { \"ocr\": true, \"columns\": false }: ocr reads images and scanned PDFs (on by default), columns reads a PDF column by column. Leave the object out for the defaults. There is no per-rule OCR switch any more, and contents.columns is only accepted from old drafts. See File contents."},{"page":"Writing rules with an AI assistant","section":"AI and automation","heading":"Names are not unique","url":"ai-authoring.html#names-are-not-unique","text":"Two rules can have the same name. When a draft or a request refers to an existing rule or preset, use its id, not its name. If several rules share a name, ask the user which one they mean. The command line exits with code 6 for an ambiguous name and lists the candidates, see Command line."},{"page":"Writing rules with an AI assistant","section":"AI and automation","heading":"Safe habits for drafts","url":"ai-authoring.html#safe-habits-for-drafts","text":"Every rule has \"enabled\": false. Use only fields in the schema. Deckhand rejects invented ones. Prefer recycle to delete, and on_conflict set to skip unless you asked for something else. Keep scripts minimal. Do not give a script credentials or network access unless you really want that. Read the assistant's list of assumptions before importing."},{"page":"Writing rules with an AI assistant","section":"AI and automation","heading":"Checking from the command line","url":"ai-authoring.html#checking-from-the-command-line","text":"AI tools that can run commands may validate a draft without importing it: Deckhand.exe --validate-draft draft.json See Command line."},{"page":"Writing rules with an AI assistant","section":"AI and automation","heading":"Limits","url":"ai-authoring.html#limits","text":"Deckhand rules react to file changes, rescans and schedules. They have no loops, no counters that persist between runs, and no \"stop processing\" step. Anything involving the network, email or databases needs a script action. If you connect a client directly instead of copying JSON, the same guide, examples, schema and catalogue are available to it as MCP resources and tools, and the write_rule prompt walks it through drafting, validating and handing over a switched-off rule. With library access it can also list its own waiting and finished requests. See Connect an AI client with MCP, Set up an AI client and the Tools reference."},{"page":"MCP overview","section":"AI and automation","heading":"MCP overview","url":"mcp.html","text":"MCP (Model Context Protocol) is a standard way for an AI client, such as Claude Desktop, Claude Code, Cursor or Codex, to start a small local program and use the tools it offers. Deckhand contains such a program. When your client starts it, the client gets a set of Deckhand tools it can call while you chat. Local MCP is a preview feature in Deckhand 0.9.50."},{"page":"MCP overview","section":"AI and automation","heading":"Why use it","url":"mcp.html#why-use-it","text":"Without MCP you can still ask any AI tool for a rule: it writes JSON, you save the file and import it (see Writing rules with an AI assistant). With MCP the same assistant can do the steps itself: read Deckhand's rule guide, examples, schema and variable catalogue (as tools or as MCP resources), so it uses only fields that exist; start from two built-in prompts, write_rule and explain_rule, in clients that show MCP prompts; check a draft with Deckhand's own validator and fix the errors before you see it; hand the finished rule to the running app, where it arrives switched off; optionally read your disabled rules, suggest edits to them and ask you to open a preview. You stay in charge. The connection cannot switch a rule on, cannot start a scan or a run by itself and cannot move or delete a file. See Safety model."},{"page":"MCP overview","section":"AI and automation","heading":"How it works","url":"mcp.html#how-it-works","text":"You add a deckhand entry to your AI client's MCP configuration. Deckhand shows the entry for you under Settings → AI integrations → Connect an AI client.... The client starts Deckhand.exe --mcp in the background when it needs it. The client and this process talk over standard input and output on your own PC. No port is opened and nothing is sent over the network by Deckhand. The process answers tool calls. The running Deckhand window stays the place where you review and decide. The AI model itself runs wherever your client runs it, usually at the provider. See What your AI provider may see."},{"page":"MCP overview","section":"AI and automation","heading":"Two kinds of connection","url":"mcp.html#two-kinds-of-connection","text":"Draft only With library access Command arguments `--mcp` `--mcp --data \"<profile folder>\" --rule-edits` Tools 4: guide, catalogue, validate, prepare 11: those 4, plus submit a rule and six tools for disabled rules and your requests Sees your rules No Names, ids, folders and definitions of eligible disabled rules Can reach the app No Yes, by queuing items you review Resources and prompts 4 resources, 2 prompts The same \"Library access\" is the name of the choice Use the library currently open in Deckhand, which is ticked by default in the setup window. The tools are described in Tools reference."},{"page":"MCP overview","section":"AI and automation","heading":"The usual workflow","url":"mcp.html#the-usual-workflow","text":"You describe the job. The assistant asks for missing paths and criteria. It reads the catalogue and writes a draft. It validates the draft and corrects errors. With library access it submits the rule, which appears in Rules switched off. Without it, it gives you prepared JSON to import yourself. You open the rule, read it and preview it on a real folder. You switch it on yourself, or run it from the preview."},{"page":"MCP overview","section":"AI and automation","heading":"Where to go next","url":"mcp.html#where-to-go-next","text":"Set up a client: Claude Desktop, Claude Code and other clients, with the exact configuration. Tools reference: every tool, its parameters and results, with an example prompt. Safety model: what a client can and cannot see or do, and privacy. Troubleshooting and FAQ. ChatGPT on the web cannot use this connection. It needs a remote server, and Deckhand's connection is a local program started by your client."},{"page":"Set up an AI client","section":"AI and automation","heading":"Set up an AI client","url":"mcp-setup.html","text":"Deckhand does not change your client's settings. You copy one entry into the client's MCP configuration yourself. This page covers what to copy and how to check it."},{"page":"Set up an AI client","section":"AI and automation","heading":"Get the entry from Deckhand","url":"mcp-setup.html#get-the-entry-from-deckhand","text":"Open Settings and find the AI integrations card. It has Connect an AI client... and View AI reference.... Click Connect an AI client.... The same window opens from Help. Choose Claude Desktop / JSON or Codex / TOML. Use the JSON entry for Claude Desktop, Cursor and most other clients. Decide on Use the library currently open in Deckhand. It is ticked by default. Click Copy setup, then paste the entry into your client's configuration. Keep any other servers that are already there. Deckhand fills in the full path of the program and, with library access, the path of the profile folder that is open right now. Use its text rather than typing your own. A screenshot of the Connect an AI client window would help here. None is included yet."},{"page":"Set up an AI client","section":"AI and automation","heading":"What the entry looks like","url":"mcp-setup.html#what-the-entry-looks-like","text":"With library access (the default): { \"mcpServers\": { \"deckhand\": { \"command\": \"C:\\\\Users\\\\ScriptKiddyMonkey\\\\AppData\\\\Local\\\\Deckhand\\\\Deckhand.exe\", \"args\": [ \"--mcp\", \"--data\", \"C:\\\\Users\\\\ScriptKiddyMonkey\\\\AppData\\\\Local\\\\Deckhand\", \"--rule-edits\" ] } } } Without library access (draft only): { \"mcpServers\": { \"deckhand\": { \"command\": \"C:\\\\Users\\\\ScriptKiddyMonkey\\\\AppData\\\\Local\\\\Deckhand\\\\Deckhand.exe\", \"args\": [\"--mcp\"] } } } The same for Codex, in config.toml: [mcp_servers.deckhand] command = \"C:\\\\Users\\\\ScriptKiddyMonkey\\\\AppData\\\\Local\\\\Deckhand\\\\Deckhand.exe\" args = [\"--mcp\", \"--data\", \"C:\\\\Users\\\\ScriptKiddyMonkey\\\\AppData\\\\Local\\\\Deckhand\", \"--rule-edits\"] The paths above are samples. Yours come from the setup window. In JSON and in TOML double-quoted strings, every backslash in a Windows path is written twice (C:\\\\Users\\\\...). A single backslash breaks the file or silently changes the path. Copying from Deckhand gives you the correct form."},{"page":"Set up an AI client","section":"AI and automation","heading":"Which program path is used","url":"mcp-setup.html#which-program-path-is-used","text":"Standalone build: Deckhand uses its stable copy, Deckhand.exe in its own application data folder, when that file exists. Otherwise it uses the exe that is running. A portable exe that you move later breaks an entry that points at the old place, so paste a fresh entry after moving it. Store build: Deckhand uses the launch alias deckhand-store.exe in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Microsoft\\WindowsApps, if that alias exists. If it does not, the setup window says that local client setup is not available in this installation. Use the standalone build for MCP until it does."},{"page":"Set up an AI client","section":"AI and automation","heading":"What --data is","url":"mcp-setup.html#what-data-is","text":"--data names the profile folder, the folder where Deckhand keeps its library. It must be an absolute path to a folder that already exists. It is what lets the connection queue items for the running app and read your disabled rules. --rule-edits is only accepted right after --data. Without --data the connection cannot reach the app at all."},{"page":"Set up an AI client","section":"AI and automation","heading":"Claude Desktop","url":"mcp-setup.html#claude-desktop","text":"Add the deckhand entry from above to the mcpServers section of Claude Desktop's MCP configuration, then quit and restart Claude Desktop. In a new chat the Deckhand tools should be listed among the available tools."},{"page":"Set up an AI client","section":"AI and automation","heading":"Claude Code","url":"mcp-setup.html#claude-code","text":"Claude Code registers a local stdio server with its own command. The program and arguments are the ones from the setup window, for example: claude mcp add deckhand -- \"C:\\Users\\ScriptKiddyMonkey\\AppData\\Local\\Deckhand\\Deckhand.exe\" --mcp --data \"C:\\Users\\ScriptKiddyMonkey\\AppData\\Local\\Deckhand\" --rule-edits Here the paths are written as in a normal PowerShell command, with single backslashes. Check the result with claude mcp list. See Claude Code's own MCP documentation for scopes and options, which Deckhand does not control."},{"page":"Set up an AI client","section":"AI and automation","heading":"Cursor, Codex and other clients","url":"mcp-setup.html#cursor-codex-and-other-clients","text":"Any client that can launch a local stdio MCP server works the same way: a command (the path to Deckhand.exe) and arguments. Use the JSON entry if the client takes mcpServers JSON, the TOML entry for Codex. Deckhand speaks MCP protocol versions 2025-11-25, 2025-06-18, 2025-03-26 and 2024-11-05. It answers with the version the client asks for, or 2025-11-25 if it asks for a version Deckhand does not know. Structured results (structuredContent) and tool titles and output schemas are sent for the two newest versions, and the same JSON is always sent as text too. Deckhand does not support JSON-RPC batches (removed from the protocol in 2025-06-18), and a request id must be a string or a number: send one message per line. A message longer than 1 MiB is answered with an error and skipped, and the connection stays open. Cancellation notifications are accepted and ignored (every tool answers at once), and nothing but JSON messages, one per line, is ever written to standard output; diagnostics go to standard error. The process ends when the client closes its input."},{"page":"Set up an AI client","section":"AI and automation","heading":"Try it","url":"mcp-setup.html#try-it","text":"Restart the client and ask: Help me organise invoice PDFs in Downloads. Ask me what you need before drafting a rule. In clients that list MCP prompts you can also pick write_rule and fill in the goal. A working connection shows the assistant calling deckhand_get_authoring_catalogue first. Then see Tools reference."},{"page":"Set up an AI client","section":"AI and automation","heading":"Test on fake files","url":"mcp-setup.html#test-on-fake-files","text":"Deckhand.exe --mcp-test is a separate mode for trying an assistant without risk. It takes no other arguments, builds a fake inbox of sample files in a temporary folder for each connection, and offers four extra deckhand_test_ tools to save a simple move rule, preview it and run it on those fake files. It never touches your real folders and has no access to your library. Register it as a second server with \"args\": [\"--mcp-test\"]."},{"page":"Set up an AI client","section":"AI and automation","heading":"See also","url":"mcp-setup.html#see-also","text":"Connect an AI client: overview, Safety model, Troubleshooting."},{"page":"MCP tools reference","section":"AI and automation","heading":"MCP tools reference","url":"mcp-tools.html","text":"A connection offers up to eleven tools, four resources and two prompts. Which tools you get depends on how the client starts Deckhand, see Set up an AI client. Tool Needs Changes anything? `deckhand_get_authoring_catalogue` always No `deckhand_get_authoring_guide` always No `deckhand_validate_draft` always No `deckhand_prepare_draft` always No `deckhand_submit_rule` `--data` Queues one new rule, switched off `deckhand_list_editable_rules` `--data --rule-edits` No `deckhand_get_editable_rule` `--data --rule-edits` No `deckhand_propose_rule_edit` `--data --rule-edits` Queues a proposal for your review `deckhand_request_rule_preview` `--data --rule-edits` Queues a request for your review `deckhand_get_request_status` `--data --rule-edits` No `deckhand_list_requests` `--data --rule-edits` No"},{"page":"MCP tools reference","section":"AI and automation","heading":"Annotations, results and errors","url":"mcp-tools.html#annotations-results-and-errors","text":"Annotations. Every tool carries MCP annotations. The seven read-only tools set readOnlyHint and idempotentHint true. The three that queue something (deckhand_submit_rule, deckhand_propose_rule_edit, deckhand_request_rule_preview) set readOnlyHint false, destructiveHint false and idempotentHint false, because every call queues another item. None of them reaches outside Deckhand (openWorldHint false). Results. Every result is JSON, sent as text. With protocol 2025-06-18 or 2025-11-25 the same JSON is also sent as structuredContent, and tools/list adds a title and a outputSchema to each tool. Older protocol versions get the plain tool list without those two fields. Cancellation and progress. Every tool answers immediately, so there is nothing to cancel or to report progress on. A notifications/cancelled message is accepted and ignored, as the protocol allows, and never gets a reply. Errors the model can fix. A failed call comes back as a normal tool result with isError: true. The JSON has valid: false or an error text, and usually a hint written for that error (a field that cannot be edited lists the editable ones; a script or command says they are not available through MCP; a stale revision says to read the rule again; a full queue says 32 requests are waiting and to review them in Deckhand). A request nested deeper than 64 levels also comes back as an error result with its own request id. Wrong or extra arguments are reported this way too, so the assistant can read the message and call again. Protocol errors. Only an unknown tool name, or a tool this connection does not offer (such as deckhand_submit_rule without --data), is a JSON-RPC error (-32602) with a message that says what to do. The connection also offers four documents as MCP resources and two prompts, for clients that support them. See Resources and prompts."},{"page":"MCP tools reference","section":"AI and automation","heading":"deckhand_get_authoring_catalogue","url":"mcp-tools.html#deckhand-get-authoring-catalogue","text":"No parameters; extra arguments are refused. Returns guide_version, variables (every built-in variable), transforms (every {id>transform} step) and draft_schema (the JSON schema of a draft). An assistant should call it before writing a draft. Example prompt: \"Which variables can a Deckhand rule use for the date a PDF was created?\""},{"page":"MCP tools reference","section":"AI and automation","heading":"deckhand_get_authoring_guide","url":"mcp-tools.html#deckhand-get-authoring-guide","text":"No parameters. Returns guide, the offline authoring guide as text. The same text is in Settings → AI integrations → View AI reference... and in the deckhand://authoring/guide resource."},{"page":"MCP tools reference","section":"AI and automation","heading":"deckhand_validate_draft","url":"mcp-tools.html#deckhand-validate-draft","text":"Parameter: draft (object, required), the draft envelope to check, for example {\"deckhand_export\":1,\"rules\":[...]}. Nothing is saved. Returns valid (true or false), errors and warnings. For drafts that do not match the schema, the result has error_details (path, code, message, allowed values) instead of plain errors, plus a hint. Validation uses the same checks as import in the app, plus limits: names up to 200 characters, text values and templates up to 4000, paths up to 1000. An absolute destination may not contain .., and every {token} in a destination or name must be a known variable (too_long, path_traversal and unknown_token in error_details). It does not look at your files. Example prompt: \"Write a rule that moves invoices from Downloads to Documents\\Invoices, and check it with Deckhand before showing it to me.\""},{"page":"MCP tools reference","section":"AI and automation","heading":"deckhand_prepare_draft","url":"mcp-tools.html#deckhand-prepare-draft","text":"Parameter: draft (object, required). Validates like the tool above. If the draft is valid, the result also contains draft: the same JSON with every rule set to enabled: false. The assistant can give you that JSON to save and import under Rules → Import. Example prompt: \"Give me a file I can import, with the rule switched off.\""},{"page":"MCP tools reference","section":"AI and automation","heading":"deckhand_submit_rule","url":"mcp-tools.html#deckhand-submit-rule","text":"Parameter: draft (object, required), a draft with exactly one rule. Returns {\"status\":\"queued\",\"id\":\"mcp_...\",\"enabled\":false} or an error result with valid: false, errors and a hint. The running app picks queued rules up within a few seconds and adds each one to Rules, switched off, with a notice. If the app is closed, the rule arrives the next time it opens this profile. \"Queued\" only means that the request was accepted. A submitted draft is refused when it: fails validation; holds more than one rule, or any preset, custom variable or script (use Rules → Import for those); contains a Run command or Run script action; finds the inbox full (32 drafts are waiting: review them in Deckhand under Rules, Review AI requests, or open the app so it can receive them). The rule is added without a group, and its enabled flag is forced off. Example prompt: \"Add that rule to Deckhand.\""},{"page":"MCP tools reference","section":"AI and automation","heading":"Reading and changing existing rules (needs --rule-edits)","url":"mcp-tools.html#reading-and-changing-existing-rules-needs-rule-edits","text":"These tools only see rules that are switched off and have no Run command or Run script action. A rule that is on, or that runs a command or script, is invisible to them and cannot be edited this way."},{"page":"MCP tools reference","section":"AI and automation","heading":"deckhand_list_editable_rules","url":"mcp-tools.html#deckhand-list-editable-rules","text":"Optional parameters: limit (1 to 200, default 50) and offset (0 or more, default 0). Returns rules (one page of eligible rules), total, next_offset (the offset of the next page, null on the last page) and a note. Each entry has rule_id, name, group, enabled (always false here), folders (the watch folder paths) and same_name_count. same_name_count counts every rule with the same name, case ignored, enabled or not. Above 1 means the name is ambiguous. Example prompt: \"Which of my Deckhand rules could you help me change?\""},{"page":"MCP tools reference","section":"AI and automation","heading":"deckhand_get_editable_rule","url":"mcp-tools.html#deckhand-get-editable-rule","text":"Parameter: rule_id (string, required), from the list, never the name. Returns rule, the full saved definition, and revision, a fingerprint of that exact saved version. Fails, with a hint to list the rules again, if the rule no longer exists, was switched on, or has a command or script action. Example prompt: \"Open the 'Invoices' rule and tell me what it does.\""},{"page":"MCP tools reference","section":"AI and automation","heading":"deckhand_propose_rule_edit","url":"mcp-tools.html#deckhand-propose-rule-edit","text":"Parameters: rule_id, revision (both required) and changes (object with at least one of name, note, folders, condition, actions). Any other field is refused. Each field you send replaces the saved field as a whole. The result must still be a valid rule using built-in variables and no command or script actions, and it must differ from the saved rule. Returns {\"id\":\"edit_...\",\"status\":\"pending_review\"}. Nothing is saved yet. The change waits under Rules → Review AI requests.... If the rule changed since the assistant read it, revision no longer matches and the proposal is refused: the assistant must read the rule again. At most 32 requests can wait at once. Example prompt: \"Change the Invoices rule so it only matches PDFs.\""},{"page":"MCP tools reference","section":"AI and automation","heading":"deckhand_request_rule_preview","url":"mcp-tools.html#deckhand-request-rule-preview","text":"Parameters: rule_id and revision (both required). Asks you to open a preview of the saved rule. Returns {\"id\":\"edit_...\",\"status\":\"pending_review\"}. The preview opens in Deckhand only after you click Open preview in the review window. It counts toward the same limit of 32. Example prompt: \"Ask me to preview the Invoices rule.\""},{"page":"MCP tools reference","section":"AI and automation","heading":"deckhand_get_request_status","url":"mcp-tools.html#deckhand-get-request-status","text":"Parameter: request_id (string, required), the id returned by a propose or preview call. Returns id and status: Status Meaning `pending_review` Waiting for you in Deckhand. `applied` You applied the edit. The rule stays disabled. `preview_handed_off` You opened the preview, or chose **Apply and preview**. `discarded` You discarded the request. `unknown` No such request, or an old one that was cleaned up. Once desktop work has started, applied and preview_handed_off can carry a work object with a phase and totals only: `phase` Extra fields `previewing`, `preview_canceled`, `preview_failed`, `running` none `preview_finished` `scanned`, `matched`, `would_fail`, `truncated` `run_finished` `done`, `skipped`, `failed`, `canceled` `run_stopped_before_execution`, `run_interrupted` none `run_recovered` `done`, `skipped`, `failed` File names, paths and messages derived from files are never included. Deckhand keeps the 64 most recent finished requests. Example prompt: \"Did I apply that edit? What did the preview find?\""},{"page":"MCP tools reference","section":"AI and automation","heading":"deckhand_list_requests","url":"mcp-tools.html#deckhand-list-requests","text":"Optional parameters: limit (1 to 200, default 50), offset (0 or more) and status (queued, pending_review, applied, preview_handed_off, discarded or rejected). Lists what this connection has put in the queue, newest first, so an assistant that lost an id can find it again and can see what is still waiting. Returns requests, total and next_offset (null on the last page), paged like deckhand_list_editable_rules. Each entry has id, kind (new_rule, edit or preview), status, updated_at (UTC, RFC 3339) and updated_at_unix. The statuses are the ones of deckhand_get_request_status, plus queued (a new rule from deckhand_submit_rule that the app has not received yet) and rejected (a queued rule the app refused). Entries for edits and previews also carry rule_id and rule_name, but only while that saved rule is still one the connection may see (switched off, no command or script action); otherwise both are null. applied and preview_handed_off entries add the same work totals as the status tool. New-rule entries show only the id, kind, status and time. Nothing else is returned: no rule contents, no paths, no file names. A request waiting in the queue is listed even if it is old; receipts follow the same limit of 64 as the status tool. Example prompt: \"What requests of yours are still waiting for me to review?\""},{"page":"MCP tools reference","section":"AI and automation","heading":"Resources and prompts","url":"mcp-tools.html#resources-and-prompts","text":"Every connection, in every mode, offers four read-only resources (resources/list, resources/read): URI Type Content `deckhand://authoring/guide` Markdown The authoring guide `deckhand://authoring/examples` Markdown Worked example drafts `deckhand://authoring/schema` JSON The JSON schema of a draft `deckhand://authoring/catalogue` JSON Built-in variables and transforms They are bundled documentation and contain nothing from your PC. Subscriptions are not supported. Two prompts (prompts/list, prompts/get) give clients a ready-made starting point, for example as slash commands: Prompt Arguments What it asks the assistant to do `write_rule` `goal` (required), `folder` Read the guide and catalogue, ask about anything ambiguous, draft with portable paths, validate, then queue the rule with `deckhand_submit_rule` (or return JSON with `deckhand_prepare_draft` when the connection cannot submit), and say plainly that the rule is switched off. `explain_rule` `rule` (required) Explain a rule in plain language and point out surprises such as overwrites, deletes and broad matches. With library access, `rule` can be a `rule_id` that the assistant reads with `deckhand_get_editable_rule`. Otherwise paste the rule JSON. Prompts only produce text for the assistant. They do not change anything."},{"page":"MCP tools reference","section":"AI and automation","heading":"Example conversation","url":"mcp-tools.html#example-conversation","text":"You: \"Make a rule that files invoice PDFs from Downloads into Documents\\Invoices.\" The assistant asks which folder and what counts as an invoice, then reads the catalogue. It validates its draft, fixes a warning, then calls deckhand_submit_rule. Deckhand shows \"Deckhand MCP added a disabled rule\". You open the rule, check it and run a preview. A screenshot of the Rules page with a rule that arrived this way would help here."},{"page":"MCP tools reference","section":"AI and automation","heading":"See also","url":"mcp-tools.html#see-also","text":"Safety model, Set up an AI client, Writing rules with an AI assistant."},{"page":"MCP safety and privacy","section":"AI and automation","heading":"MCP safety and privacy","url":"mcp-safety.html","text":"The design rule is simple: the AI proposes and Deckhand's window decides. Every change that matters goes through a review you open yourself."},{"page":"MCP safety and privacy","section":"AI and automation","heading":"Why imported rules arrive switched off","url":"mcp-safety.html#why-imported-rules-arrive-switched-off","text":"A rule written by a program can look right and still match more files than you meant. A rule that is on starts working as soon as a file appears in its folder. So a rule from an assistant is always added with enabled off: deckhand_prepare_draft and deckhand_submit_rule force it, and an edit applied from a request leaves the rule off too. You read it, preview it on a real folder and switch it on yourself."},{"page":"MCP safety and privacy","section":"AI and automation","heading":"The draft workflow","url":"mcp-safety.html#the-draft-workflow","text":"Author: the assistant reads the catalogue and writes a draft. Validate: deckhand_validate_draft reports errors and warnings. The assistant fixes them. Import switched off: deckhand_submit_rule (or import of the JSON from deckhand_prepare_draft). Preview: you open the rule's preview in Deckhand. See Preview, run and undo. Enable: you switch the rule on, or run from the preview."},{"page":"MCP safety and privacy","section":"AI and automation","heading":"What a connected client can do","url":"mcp-safety.html#what-a-connected-client-can-do","text":"It can It cannot Read the authoring guide, examples, schema and variable catalogue Read, list, move, copy or delete any file on your PC Validate drafts and prepare JSON Switch a rule on, or save a rule as enabled With `--data`: queue one new rule, which arrives switched off Submit rules that run a command or a script, presets, custom variables or scripts With `--rule-edits`: list and read rules that are off and have no command or script action See rules that are switched on, or rules with a command or script action Queue an edit or a preview request for your review Approve its own request, apply an edit, or start a scan or run Ask for the status of its own requests, or list them (`deckhand_list_requests`) See file names or paths from a preview or run, only totals Change Deckhand's settings, schedules, presets or variables The request list shows only the connection's own queue entries: id, kind, status, time and, for a rule it may already read, its id and name. It never names a rule that is switched on or runs a command or script. What a client reads with library access is exactly this, per rule: id, name, group and watch folder paths in the list and, when it reads one rule, the whole definition including conditions, actions, destination paths and notes. Names can repeat, so every call uses the id, and same_name_count tells the assistant when it must ask you which rule you mean."},{"page":"MCP safety and privacy","section":"AI and automation","heading":"Review and the run gate","url":"mcp-safety.html#review-and-the-run-gate","text":"A proposed edit or preview request waits under Rules → Review AI requests.... You see the current and the proposed rule side by side in plain language, with the raw fields under Technical details. Choices: Apply edit, Apply and preview (saves, keeps the rule off and opens the preview), Discard or Review later. For a preview request: Open preview. A proposal is refused if the rule changed, was switched on or no longer exists since the assistant read it. A preview opened this way is a normal Deckhand preview. A real run from it always asks for your confirmation, even if you turned run confirmations off. The preview expires after ten minutes, can be used once, is rebuilt and compared before the run, and is refused if the files or proposed operations differ. Live and scheduled work wait while the check and run happen. Runs from such a preview are recorded in History and can be undone like any other run, with the source shown as MCP."},{"page":"MCP safety and privacy","section":"AI and automation","heading":"Limits that protect you","url":"mcp-safety.html#limits-that-protect-you","text":"A message from the client may be at most 1 MiB. A longer one is rejected and skipped; the connection stays open. deckhand_list_editable_rules returns at most 200 rules per call. Prompts and resources are fixed text. They never read your rules or files. At most 32 submitted drafts and 32 review requests can wait at once. Drafts that fail validation are never queued. A queued file that turns out invalid when the app reads it is set aside. Scripts and commands never arrive through MCP."},{"page":"MCP safety and privacy","section":"AI and automation","heading":"Privacy","url":"mcp-safety.html#privacy","text":"Deckhand's connection itself makes no network connection. It runs on your PC, started by your client. But the model that powers your client usually does not run on your PC, and everything the client reads can become part of what it sends to its provider. What your AI provider may see: Anything you type in the chat. Tool results: the catalogue and guide (not private), validation messages, and the content of drafts it writes. With library access, for eligible disabled rules: names, group, folder paths, conditions, actions, destination paths, notes and text you wrote in them. Aggregate totals (files scanned, matched, done, failed) of previews and runs you handed off. It does not see file names or file contents, because the connection cannot read your files. Choose a client and an account whose data policy you accept. Clear Use the library currently open in Deckhand if you do not want rule details to leave your PC. The assistant can still help you write and validate rules. For Deckhand's own data practices see the privacy policy. Do not paste secrets such as passwords into a chat or a rule note. Anything in a rule definition that the client reads can reach its provider."},{"page":"MCP safety and privacy","section":"AI and automation","heading":"See also","url":"mcp-safety.html#see-also","text":"Tools reference, Set up an AI client, Troubleshooting."},{"page":"MCP troubleshooting and FAQ","section":"AI and automation","heading":"MCP troubleshooting and FAQ","url":"mcp-troubleshooting.html","text":""},{"page":"MCP troubleshooting and FAQ","section":"AI and automation","heading":"The client does not connect","url":"mcp-troubleshooting.html#the-client-does-not-connect","text":"Make sure the client was fully restarted after you edited its configuration. Check that command points at a file that exists. Paste the path into the Windows Run box or open it in Explorer. If you moved a portable Deckhand.exe, the old path in the entry is dead. Open Connect an AI client... again and paste the new entry. Run the program yourself in PowerShell: & \"C:\\Users\\ScriptKiddyMonkey\\AppData\\Local\\Deckhand\\Deckhand.exe\" --mcp. It waits silently for input, which is normal. Press Ctrl+C to leave. Wrong arguments print Use --mcp, optionally followed by --data <existing absolute profile path> [--rule-edits]. and end with exit code 2. --data must be an absolute path to a folder that exists, and --rule-edits must come right after it. Security software can block a program that a client starts. Allow Deckhand.exe."},{"page":"MCP troubleshooting and FAQ","section":"AI and automation","heading":"Tools are missing","url":"mcp-troubleshooting.html#tools-are-missing","text":"You only see the four authoring tools: the entry has no --data. The setup window adds it when Use the library currently open in Deckhand is ticked. You see five tools: the entry has --data but not --rule-edits. With library access you should see eleven. The client caches its tool list. Restart the client or start a new chat. deckhand_list_editable_rules returns an empty list: only rules that are switched off and have no command or script action are listed. A rule you switched on is not visible."},{"page":"MCP troubleshooting and FAQ","section":"AI and automation","heading":"Windows path and quote problems","url":"mcp-troubleshooting.html#windows-path-and-quote-problems","text":"JSON and TOML: write each backslash twice. \"C:\\\\Users\\\\ScriptKiddyMonkey\\\\AppData\\\\Local\\\\Deckhand\\\\Deckhand.exe\". On a command line (for example claude mcp add), quote paths that contain spaces and write single backslashes. Keep every path in args as its own list item. Do not put the whole line into command. Use the full path, not just Deckhand.exe: a client may not search your PATH. On a command line do not end a quoted path with a lone backslash before the closing quote (\"C:\\Folder\\\")."},{"page":"MCP troubleshooting and FAQ","section":"AI and automation","heading":"Version mismatch","url":"mcp-troubleshooting.html#version-mismatch","text":"The entry points at one Deckhand.exe, and that is the version the tools come from. If you update Deckhand to a new place or version and keep an old entry, the client talks to the old program. After an update or a move, copy a fresh entry from Connect an AI client.... In View AI reference..., the MCP tools tab lists the tools of the version you have installed."},{"page":"MCP troubleshooting and FAQ","section":"AI and automation","heading":"A tool call comes back as an error","url":"mcp-troubleshooting.html#a-tool-call-comes-back-as-an-error","text":"Failed calls are normal tool results marked as errors, usually with a hint. Typical ones: Invalid arguments for ...: the assistant sent a wrong or extra argument. It should read the tool's schema and call again. No such editable rule: the rule is gone, switched on, or has a command or script. List the rules again. -32602 ... deckhand_submit_rule is only offered when Deckhand is started with --data: the entry has no --data. Copy a fresh entry with the library box ticked, or use deckhand_prepare_draft. Unknown argument ... or limit must be between 1 and 200: a list tool got an argument it does not have or a value out of range. The work result file could not be understood: the app wrote progress this server version does not know. The decision is still correct; check the progress in Deckhand. -32002 Server not initialized: the client sent a request before finishing the MCP handshake. This is a client problem; restart the client."},{"page":"MCP troubleshooting and FAQ","section":"AI and automation","heading":"A request shows an error such as \"The saved rule changed\"","url":"mcp-troubleshooting.html#a-request-shows-an-error-such-as-the-saved-rule-changed","text":"The rule was changed after the assistant read it. Ask the assistant to read the rule again and propose the edit again."},{"page":"MCP troubleshooting and FAQ","section":"AI and automation","heading":"\"Review AI requests\" shows nothing","url":"mcp-troubleshooting.html#review-ai-requests-shows-nothing","text":"The request may have been discarded or may not have reached the profile that is open. With --data, the profile folder in the entry must be the one Deckhand is using now. If you use more than one profile or a portable copy, check the folder."},{"page":"MCP troubleshooting and FAQ","section":"AI and automation","heading":"The rule I submitted did not appear","url":"mcp-troubleshooting.html#the-rule-i-submitted-did-not-appear","text":"Queued rules arrive when the running app reads its inbox, which happens within a few seconds while the app is open. If Deckhand is closed, open it. If the draft was invalid when the app read it, it is set aside and never added."},{"page":"MCP troubleshooting and FAQ","section":"AI and automation","heading":"FAQ","url":"mcp-troubleshooting.html#faq","text":"Does the AI see my files? No. The connection has no tool that reads, lists or searches files. See Safety model. Can the AI run a rule? No. It can ask you to open a preview. A run happens only if you confirm it in Deckhand. Why is a rule I know about missing from the list? Only rules that are off and without a command or script are visible to the client. Can it write rules with scripts or Run command? It can draft them for you to import by hand, but it cannot submit or edit such rules through the connection. Do I need an account or internet for Deckhand's part? No. Your client may need both for its model. Is ChatGPT supported? Not on the web. It needs a remote MCP server, and Deckhand only offers a local program. Does it work in the Store edition? Only if the installation has the deckhand-store.exe alias. If it does not, the setup window tells you. Can I turn it off? Remove the deckhand entry from the client. Deckhand has no always-running MCP service."},{"page":"MCP troubleshooting and FAQ","section":"AI and automation","heading":"See also","url":"mcp-troubleshooting.html#see-also","text":"Set up an AI client, Tools reference, Troubleshooting."},{"page":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","section":"Help","heading":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","url":"troubleshooting.html","text":""},{"page":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","section":"Help","heading":"A file was not moved","url":"troubleshooting.html#a-file-was-not-moved","text":"Work down this list. The first screenshot is Operations on the Home page, which lists the rules that need attention, what is wrong with each and a Retry rule button where retrying helps. The second, further down, is the Log with its result filter set to Failed. It lists the failed action with the reason in the Result details column. The Log filtered to failed lines: Is the rule on? Check its switch and, if it is in a group, that the group is on. Is the file still changing? Deckhand waits until a file has been unchanged for 15 seconds by default. Files ending in .crdownload, .part or .tmp, locked files and online-only cloud files are left alone. A preview lists them under Not ready. Is it hidden, system or read-only? These are skipped unless you allow them in Settings. Did the rule handle it already? Each rule handles a file once per modified time. Saving a rule clears its memory, so it looks again. Is it excluded? Check Exclusions and the preview's Excluded tab. Is it already in a destination folder? Ignore files already in the folders this rule moves to is on by default. Did an earlier rule move it? Rules run top to bottom, and the next rule sees the file where the first left it. Use Simulate to follow it. Does the condition really match? Use Preview and look at No match. Remember that unfinished rows never match. For a File contents condition, open its gear and choose Show what Deckhand reads from a file... to see the text Deckhand really gets from that file. A scan or picture with no text of its own is only read when OCR is ticked. Is the PC on battery? Pause rules on battery in Settings makes rules wait until the PC is plugged in. The rule status says so."},{"page":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","section":"Help","heading":"OCR says it is not available","url":"troubleshooting.html#ocr-says-it-is-not-available","text":"A File contents condition or variable reads text from pictures and scanned PDFs through the OCR built into Windows. When it cannot, the gear of that condition shows a message instead of just ticking the option: Windows has no OCR language installed, so pictures and scans cannot be read. Add a language in Windows Settings under Time & language, Language & region, and make sure its Optical character recognition feature is installed. Then reopen the gear. The Language list in Settings, Text in pictures and scans (OCR), then shows it. The OCR language ... chosen in Settings is not installed in Windows. Choose another language in the Language list of that card, or install the one you chose. Empty means the languages of your Windows profile. ... which is not available on this system. Windows OCR is only available on Windows. Clear OCR cache on the same card forgets the text Deckhand already read from pictures. Use it after you change the language. OCR can misread. Files matched only by recognised text are marked in the preview, and a file is never deleted, recycled or overwritten because of such a match unless you approve it. Text files, PDFs with text and Office files do not need OCR at all."},{"page":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","section":"Help","heading":"Two rules or presets have the same name","url":"troubleshooting.html#two-rules-or-presets-have-the-same-name","text":"Names are not unique. Deckhand warns when you save a rule or preset with a name that already exists (A rule called ... already exists. Save anyway?) and lets you save it, so you can end up with two of the same name. Rename one of them so you can tell them apart. In the Rules page the group and the watched folders tell them apart too. The command line and AI clients use the id, which is always unique. If you give a name that matches several rules or presets, the command line does nothing, lists the candidates with their ids and exits with code 6. Run it again with an id. See Command line."},{"page":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","section":"Help","heading":"A rule needs attention","url":"troubleshooting.html#a-rule-needs-attention","text":"Open the rule and read its status line. Common reasons: a folder that no longer exists, a destination Deckhand cannot write to, or a step that failed. Failed files show in History → Problems with the reason."},{"page":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","section":"Help","heading":"Windows SmartScreen shows a warning","url":"troubleshooting.html#windows-smartscreen-shows-a-warning","text":"The portable GitHub download is unsigned, so SmartScreen may show an unknown-publisher warning. Compare the file's SHA-256 with the checksum published on the release before running it. The Microsoft Store version is signed."},{"page":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","section":"Help","heading":"Deckhand does not start or shows a blank window","url":"troubleshooting.html#deckhand-does-not-start-or-shows-a-blank-window","text":"Deckhand needs the Microsoft Edge WebView2 Runtime. Install the Evergreen Runtime from Microsoft and try again."},{"page":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","section":"Help","heading":"Undo did not put everything back","url":"troubleshooting.html#undo-did-not-put-everything-back","text":"Undo restores files as far as it still can. A file you changed after the run is left alone and reported. Permanent delete and the effects of commands and scripts cannot be undone. See Preview, Run and Undo."},{"page":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","section":"Help","heading":"A rule keeps running on the same files","url":"troubleshooting.html#a-rule-keeps-running-on-the-same-files","text":"Deckhand records what a rule created and the file's new modified time so copies inside a watched folder do not trigger the rule again. If you still see loops, narrow the conditions or send the output outside the watched folder."},{"page":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","section":"Help","heading":"Two rules fight over a file","url":"troubleshooting.html#two-rules-fight-over-a-file","text":"Make their conditions exclusive. There is no \"stop processing\" step. For if/else behaviour, give the second rule the opposite condition using None of or a not group."},{"page":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","section":"Help","heading":"FAQ","url":"troubleshooting.html#faq","text":"Is Deckhand free? Yes, for personal and commercial use. It is in beta. Does it send my files anywhere? No. Deckhand does not require an account, send telemetry, or upload your files. Scripts you write and AI clients you connect have their own behaviour. See Privacy. Can I move my rules to another PC? Yes. In Settings, export your library and settings, then restore or import on the new PC. Imported rules start disabled so you can check their paths first. Can I run a rule once without watching? Yes. Use Preview and Run, or run a preset on a folder. Where do I report a bug? Use the issue form. Issues are public, so remove personal file names and paths from anything you attach."},{"page":"Privacy","section":"Help","heading":"Privacy","url":"privacy.html","text":"The full text is the Deckhand privacy policy. This page is a summary, not a replacement."},{"page":"Privacy","section":"Help","heading":"In short","url":"privacy.html#in-short","text":"Deckhand processes your files on your device. It may read names, paths, metadata and contents, depending on your rules. It does not require an account, send telemetry, or upload your files, file contents or logs to the publisher. Settings, rules, history, logs and recovery data are stored locally. History and logs can contain file names and paths. Anyone with access to the data folder can read them. You choose which folders Deckhand processes. You can disable rules, choose per File contents condition whether pictures and scans are read with OCR, and set how long history is kept."},{"page":"Privacy","section":"Help","heading":"Things you add","url":"privacy.html#things-you-add","text":"Cloud folders: if you enable online-only downloads, Deckhand can request files through your sync provider, such as OneDrive. Your provider's policy applies to what it syncs. Commands and scripts: a command or script you configure can do whatever it is written to do, with the permissions it runs with. Review scripts before enabling them. AI clients: if you connect a client and give it library access, it can read eligible rule definitions, which can contain folder paths. The client may share what it reads with its model provider. You can connect without library access."},{"page":"Privacy","section":"Help","heading":"Questions","url":"privacy.html#questions","text":"Use the support page. GitHub issues are public, so leave out personal paths and names."}];
